/* window.DRILLS — {id, kind: quant|concept|design|debug, level, module, q, a}
   Answers are split on blank lines; a paragraph fenced with ``` renders as code. */
(function () {
  var D = [];
  function d(id, kind, level, module, q, a) {
    D.push({ id: id, kind: kind, level: level, module: module, q: q, a: a });
  }

  /* ---------------- Module 00: ground truth ---------------- */
  d('dr-001', 'quant', 'easy', '00 Ground truth',
    'Llama 3.1 8B in BF16 on one H100 SXM. Batch 1, context 4096, assume 80% of peak bandwidth. What is the decode token rate, and what fraction of the tensor cores are you using?',
    'Bytes moved per step = weights + live KV.\n\n```\nweights = 8.03e9 params * 2 B      = 16.06 GB\nKV/token = 2*32*8*128*2            = 131,072 B = 128 KiB\nKV live  = 131,072 * 4096          = 0.537 GB\ntotal                              = 16.60 GB\n```\n\nAt 3.35 TB/s * 0.8 = 2.68 TB/s: `t_step = 16.60e9 / 2.68e12 = 6.19 ms`, so about **161 tok/s**.\n\nFLOPs per step = `2 * 8.03e9 = 16.06 GFLOP`. Achieved rate = `16.06e9 / 6.19e-3 = 2.59 TFLOP/s` against a 989 TFLOP/s roof, i.e. **MFU = 0.26%**.\n\nThat number is not a bug. It is the entire reason batching exists, and the reason chasing MFU during decode is a category error. Report MBU (80% here) instead.'
  );

  d('dr-002', 'quant', 'easy', '00 Ground truth',
    'Compute KV bytes per token for Llama 3.1 70B in FP16, and the KV cache for one request at 128k context. How does that compare with the weights?',
    '```\nKV/token = 2 * L * n_kv * d_head * bytes\n         = 2 * 80 * 8 * 128 * 2 = 327,680 B = 320 KiB\n```\n\nAt 131,072 tokens: `327,680 * 131,072 = 42.9 GB` = **40.0 GiB**.\n\nWeights in FP16 are `70.6e9 * 2 = 141.2 GB`. So a *single* 128k request costs 30% of the weight footprint. Eight such requests exceed the weights.\n\nThis is why long-context serving is a memory-capacity problem long before it is a compute problem, and why KV quantisation and MLA get so much attention.'
  );

  d('dr-003', 'quant', 'medium', '00 Ground truth',
    'Derive the ridge point of an H100 SXM in BF16, then derive the batch size at which BF16 decode becomes compute bound. Repeat for FP8 weights. Why does the answer move in the direction it does?',
    '```\nridge = 989e12 FLOP/s / 3.35e12 B/s = 295 FLOP/byte\n```\n\nDecode arithmetic intensity ignoring KV is `AI = 2*N*B / (N * w_bytes) = 2B / w_bytes`.\n\nBF16 (`w_bytes = 2`): `AI = B`, so `B_crit = 295`.\nFP8 (`w_bytes = 1`): `AI = 2B`, so `B_crit = 148`.\n\nQuantising the weights **halves** the critical batch size. You moved bytes down without moving FLOPs down, so every token now carries twice the arithmetic intensity and you hit the compute roof sooner.\n\nThe practical consequence is counterintuitive and important: W4A16 gives you a big win at batch 1 and essentially nothing at batch 512, because by then you are on the compute roof where the weight precision no longer matters.'
  );

  d('dr-004', 'quant', 'hard', '00 Ground truth',
    'At what prompt length do the quadratic attention FLOPs equal the linear projection FLOPs during prefill, for Llama 3.1 8B and for Llama 3.1 70B? What does this tell you about when the `2N` rule of thumb stops working?',
    'Linear term: `2 * N_params * S`. Attention term: `4 * L * S^2 * d_model` (QK^T and AV, each `2 * S^2 * d_model` per layer).\n\nSet equal and solve:\n\n```\n2 * N * S = 4 * L * S^2 * d\nS* = N / (2 * L * d)\n\n8B : 8.03e9 / (2 * 32 * 4096)  = 30,600 tokens\n70B: 70.6e9 / (2 * 80 * 8192)  = 53,900 tokens\n```\n\nBelow roughly 30k tokens, `2N` per token is a good approximation and your prefill cost is linear in prompt length. Above it, prefill cost grows superlinearly and doubling the prompt more than doubles TTFT.\n\nNote that bigger models push the crossover *out*, because `N` grows faster than `L*d`. Long-context serving is relatively harder on small models.'
  );

  d('dr-005', 'quant', 'medium', '00 Ground truth',
    'A request has a 4000-token prompt and generates 500 tokens on one H100 running Llama 3.1 8B. Estimate TTFT and end-to-end latency, and state the prefill:decode split of both FLOPs and wall time. Assume 45% MFU on prefill and 80% MBU on decode.',
    '```\nprefill FLOPs = 2 * 8.03e9 * 4000 = 64.2 TFLOP\nt_prefill     = 64.2e12 / (989e12 * 0.45) = 144 ms\n\nt_decode/step ~ 6.2 ms (from drill 01, KV grows to ~4.5k tokens)\nt_decode      = 500 * 6.2 ms = 3.10 s\n```\n\nTTFT is about **144 ms** (plus queue). E2E is about **3.25 s**.\n\nFLOPs: prefill 64.2 TFLOP, decode `2*8.03e9*500 = 8.0 TFLOP`. Prefill is **89%** of the arithmetic.\n\nWall time: prefill is **4.4%**. Decode is 95.6%.\n\nThat inversion is the single most important fact in this field. Almost all the arithmetic happens in prefill; almost all the time is spent in decode. Optimise accordingly.'
  );

  d('dr-006', 'quant', 'medium', '00 Ground truth',
    'You have one H100 80GB and want to serve Llama 3.1 8B in BF16 with 32k context per request. Set `gpu_memory_utilization` to 0.90. How many concurrent requests fit?',
    '```\nusable      = 80 * 0.90        = 72.0 GB\nweights                          = 16.06 GB\nactivations + CUDA ctx + buffers ~ 3.0 GB\nKV budget                        = 52.9 GB\n\nKV per request = 131,072 * 32,768 = 4.295 GB\nB_max          = 52.9 / 4.295     = 12 requests\n```\n\nTwelve. On a GPU whose compute roof is not reached until batch 295.\n\nYou are memory-capacity bound by a factor of 25. The levers, in order of effect: KV quantisation to FP8 (24 requests), shorter effective context via a smaller `max_model_len` (linear), prefix caching if the traffic shares prefixes, offloading to host memory, and finally more GPUs.'
  );

  d('dr-007', 'concept', 'medium', '00 Ground truth',
    'Your decode throughput scales almost linearly from batch 1 to batch 64, then flattens hard at batch 96 — far below the critical batch size of 295. What happened?',
    'You ran out of KV cache, not compute.\n\nThroughput scales linearly while the weight read is amortised across the batch and the KV traffic is small. It stops scaling when one of three things happens: (a) you hit `B_crit` and become compute bound, (b) the KV cache fills and the scheduler stops admitting or starts preempting, or (c) the KV traffic itself becomes the dominant byte term.\n\nAt batch 96 with a long context, (b) and (c) are the candidates. Check `vllm:num_preemptions_total` and `vllm:gpu_cache_usage_perc`. If cache usage is pinned at ~100% and preemptions are nonzero, it is (b).\n\nFor (c): KV bytes per step = `B * S_ctx * kv_bytes_per_token`. At B=96, S=8192, 128 KiB/token that is 100 GB per step against 16 GB of weights. The KV traffic is now 86% of all bytes moved, so adding batch adds proportional bytes and throughput per request stops improving.'
  );

  d('dr-008', 'concept', 'easy', '00 Ground truth',
    'Why is the FLOPs-per-token cost of inference `2N` while training is `6N`?',
    'Forward pass: each parameter participates in one multiply and one accumulate, so `2N`.\n\nBackward pass computes two gradients per parameter — the gradient with respect to the input activations and the gradient with respect to the weights — each costing about the same as the forward matmul, so `4N`.\n\nTotal training `= 2N + 4N = 6N`.\n\nInference never runs the backward pass, hence `2N`. The corollary worth internalising: one training step over a token costs the same as three inference forward passes, so a model that is served heavily spends far more compute in inference over its lifetime than it did in training.'
  );

  d('dr-009', 'quant', 'hard', '00 Ground truth',
    'Compare the KV cache of a hypothetical 7B model using full MHA (32 heads, d_head 128, 32 layers) with Llama 3.1 8B using GQA-8, and with DeepSeek-V3 using MLA. Rank them per token, then comment on the fairness of the comparison.',
    '```\nMHA 7B      : 2 * 32 * 32 * 128 * 2 =  524,288 B = 512 KiB\nGQA-8 (8B)  : 2 * 32 *  8 * 128 * 2 =  131,072 B = 128 KiB\nMLA (V3)    :     61 * 576 * 2      =   70,272 B =  68.6 KiB\n```\n\nGQA-8 is a clean 4x reduction over MHA at essentially the same parameter count. MLA is a further 1.9x — on a model that is 84x larger.\n\nThe comparison is not entirely fair in either direction. MLA has no factor of 2, because the latent is a single tensor from which both K and V are reconstructed; that reconstruction is extra FLOPs at decode time, traded against bytes. And DeepSeek-V3 is an MoE, so its 671B parameters activate only 37B per token, which changes the weight-read term completely.\n\nThe general lesson: KV footprint is an architecture decision made before you ever see the model, and it dominates your serving economics for the model\u2019s entire life.'
  );

  d('dr-010', 'design', 'hard', '00 Ground truth',
    'Two products share one GPU pool: a document summariser (average 8000 in, 200 out) and a reasoning assistant (average 300 in, 4000 out). Traffic is 50/50 by request count. Explain what will go wrong and what you would do about it.',
    'The two workloads sit at opposite ends of the roofline and fight each other.\n\nThe summariser is prefill dominated: `2*N*8000` FLOPs of compute-bound work per request, 200 decode steps. It wants a large token budget per iteration and a high MFU. The assistant is decode dominated: 4000 memory-bound steps holding KV the whole time. It wants a large batch and stable TPOT.\n\nOn one pool the summariser\u2019s prefills cause generation stalls in the assistant\u2019s decodes, so assistant TPOT p99 spikes. Meanwhile the assistant\u2019s long-lived sequences occupy KV blocks for minutes, shrinking the KV budget and forcing preemptions that hit the summariser\u2019s TTFT.\n\nFixes, in increasing order of cost:\n\n1. **Chunked prefill with a tuned token budget.** Caps the damage a single 8000-token prefill can do to one iteration. Cheapest, works immediately.\n2. **Separate the pools by workload.** Route on prompt length or on an explicit product tag. Different `max_num_batched_tokens`, different `max_num_seqs`, different SLOs.\n3. **Prefill/decode disaggregation.** Give prefill high tensor parallelism for latency and decode high batch for throughput; this is exactly the case DistServe and Splitwise were written for.\n\nWhichever you choose, define separate SLOs per workload first. A single p99 latency target over a bimodal workload is meaningless.'
  );

  /* ---------------- Module 01: kernels ---------------- */
  d('dr-011', 'quant', 'medium', '01 Kernels',
    'For S=4096, d_head=128, one head: compute the HBM traffic of a naive attention implementation that materialises the score matrix, and of FlashAttention. Assume FP16 and ignore reads that hit in L2.',
    '```\nQ, K, V each: 4096 * 128 * 2 B = 1.05 MB   -> 3.15 MB in\nS = QK^T    : 4096 * 4096 * 2  = 33.6 MB      write\nread back for softmax                          33.6 MB\nwrite P                                        33.6 MB\nread P for PV                                  33.6 MB\nO out                                           1.05 MB\nnaive total  ~ 138 MB\n```\n\nFlashAttention never materialises S. It reads Q, K, V once and writes O once: about **4.2 MB**, plus the log-sum-exp statistics.\n\nThat is a **33x** reduction in HBM traffic for one head at this length, and the ratio grows linearly with S. The FLOPs are identical — FlashAttention actually does slightly *more* arithmetic because of the rescaling — which is the whole point: on a memory-bound operation you trade FLOPs for bytes and win.'
  );

  d('dr-012', 'concept', 'hard', '01 Kernels',
    'Derive the online softmax rescaling update. Why is it numerically safe, and what exactly does FlashAttention carry between tiles?',
    'Standard safe softmax needs the global max, which needs a full pass. Online softmax maintains a running max `m` and a running sum `l` and corrects retroactively.\n\nAfter processing tiles up to `j`, with `m_j = max(m_{j-1}, max(x_j))`:\n\n```\nl_j = l_{j-1} * exp(m_{j-1} - m_j) + sum(exp(x_j - m_j))\nO_j = O_{j-1} * exp(m_{j-1} - m_j) + exp(x_j - m_j) @ V_j\n```\n\nThe factor `exp(m_{j-1} - m_j)` rewrites everything accumulated so far as if it had always been normalised by the new max. Since `m_{j-1} <= m_j`, that exponent is never positive, so the correction factor is in (0, 1] and cannot overflow. Every exponential argument is `<= 0`. That is the numerical safety argument, and it is exact, not approximate.\n\nBetween tiles FlashAttention carries three things per query row: the running output accumulator `O`, the running sum `l`, and the running max `m`. All three live in SRAM. The final output is `O / l`.\n\nThe backward pass recomputes the scores from the stored `(m, l)` rather than reading a saved S matrix — recomputation is cheaper than the memory traffic it avoids.'
  );

  d('dr-013', 'concept', 'medium', '01 Kernels',
    'Why is the FlashAttention prefill kernel a poor choice for decode, and what does Flash-Decoding change?',
    'FlashAttention parallelises over batch, heads, and *query blocks*. During decode there is exactly one query per sequence, so the query-block dimension collapses to 1.\n\nParallelism available = `batch * n_heads`. At batch 1 with 32 heads that is 32 thread blocks on a GPU with 132 SMs. You are using 24% of the machine and the KV read — which is the expensive part — is serialised within each block.\n\nFlash-Decoding splits the **KV length** into chunks, computes partial attention outputs and partial log-sum-exp statistics for each chunk in parallel, then combines them with the same rescaling identity used across tiles. Parallelism becomes `batch * n_heads * n_kv_splits`, which saturates the GPU even at batch 1.\n\nThe cost is a second reduction kernel and some extra memory for the partials. At long context the win is several times; at short context it is a loss, which is why engines dispatch on sequence length.'
  );

  d('dr-014', 'debug', 'hard', '01 Kernels',
    'A colleague reports that `torch.nn.functional.scaled_dot_product_attention` is 6x slower than expected on their new model. Nothing in the model looks unusual. How do you diagnose it in three steps?',
    '1. **Check which backend was dispatched.** Wrap the call in `torch.backends.cuda.sdp_kernel` context or use `torch.nn.attention.sdpa_kernel` to force each backend in turn. If forcing FLASH raises an error, that error message is your answer. Common causes: head_dim above the supported limit, an unsupported dtype (FP32), a non-`None` attn_mask that is not a simple causal flag, a non-contiguous or oddly strided input, or dropout in eval.\n\n2. **Profile it.** In a torch profiler trace, the math fallback shows up as separate `bmm`, `softmax` and `bmm` kernels with a huge intermediate allocation. The flash path is a single fused kernel. This is unambiguous.\n\n3. **Check the mask.** Passing an explicit boolean causal mask instead of `is_causal=True` silently disables the flash backend in several versions, and also forces the kernel to materialise and read an S-shaped mask. This is by far the most common cause in practice.\n\nThe general lesson: SDPA is a dispatcher, not a kernel. Silent fallback to the math implementation is the single most common source of unexplained attention slowness.'
  );

  d('dr-015', 'concept', 'medium', '01 Kernels',
    'You profile a decode server and find GPU utilisation reported at 95%, but summed kernel time in the Nsight timeline is only 40% of wall clock. What is the bottleneck and what fixes it?',
    'You are overhead bound. `nvidia-smi` utilisation means "at least one kernel was resident during the sampling interval", not "the SMs were busy", so it is nearly useless for this diagnosis.\n\nThe missing 60% is host-side: Python scheduling, per-kernel launch overhead (5-10 us each), the sampler, detokenisation, the HTTP layer, and synchronisation points. With a 6 ms decode step and 300 kernel launches, launch overhead alone can be 2-3 ms.\n\nFixes, in order:\n\n1. **CUDA graphs.** Capture the whole decode step as one replayable graph. This removes launch overhead almost entirely and is why every serious engine uses them for decode.\n2. **Move the scheduler off the critical path.** SGLang overlaps the CPU scheduler with GPU execution; vLLM V1 moved the engine core into its own process for the same reason.\n3. **Remove synchronisation.** Any `.item()`, `.cpu()`, or Python-side conditional on a tensor value inserts a sync. The sampler is the usual offender.\n4. **Batch the detokenisation** and stream asynchronously.\n\nNote that overhead-bound is the third regime alongside compute- and memory-bound, and it is the only one you cannot detect from arithmetic alone. You need a timeline.'
  );

  d('dr-016', 'quant', 'medium', '01 Kernels',
    'A decode step launches 380 kernels and takes 6.2 ms. Kernel launch overhead is 6 us. What fraction of the step is launch overhead, and what does CUDA graph capture buy you?',
    '```\nlaunch overhead = 380 * 6 us = 2.28 ms\nfraction        = 2.28 / 6.2  = 37%\n```\n\nCUDA graphs replace 380 individual launches with one graph launch of roughly 10-20 us, recovering nearly all 2.28 ms. New step time about 3.95 ms, a **1.57x** speedup with no change to any kernel.\n\nCaveats you must know: the graph captures fixed shapes and fixed pointers, so engines capture a set of graphs at bucketed batch sizes and pad up to the nearest bucket. That padding is wasted work, which is why vLLM V1 uses piecewise capture — graphing the shape-stable parts and leaving attention, whose shapes vary with sequence length, outside.\n\nAlso note this ratio gets *worse* as your model gets faster. A 2x kernel speedup makes overhead 54% of the step.'
  );

  d('dr-017', 'concept', 'hard', '01 Kernels',
    'Explain why FlashAttention-3 needs warp specialisation and TMA on Hopper, when FlashAttention-2 was already close to hardware limits on Ampere.',
    'On Ampere the tensor cores and the memory pipeline are close enough in speed that a single warp doing load-then-compute keeps both reasonably busy. On Hopper the tensor cores got roughly 3x faster while HBM bandwidth grew about 1.6x, so a synchronous load-compute loop leaves the tensor cores idle waiting on data.\n\nFA3 addresses this three ways:\n\n1. **Warp specialisation (producer/consumer).** Some warps do nothing but issue TMA loads into shared memory; others do nothing but consume from shared memory into the tensor cores. They synchronise through an asynchronous barrier. This decouples the two pipelines so a stall in one does not stall the other.\n\n2. **TMA (Tensor Memory Accelerator).** A dedicated hardware unit that performs multidimensional async copies without consuming register or instruction issue slots. It makes the producer warps nearly free.\n\n3. **Ping-pong scheduling of GEMM and softmax.** The softmax is exponentials on the special function units, not the tensor cores. FA3 overlaps the softmax of one block with the GEMM of the next, so the non-matmul work stops being on the critical path — this was the largest single remaining inefficiency in FA2.\n\nFP8 support adds block quantisation plus incoherent processing (a Hadamard rotation) to control outliers, which is the same trick QuaRot uses on weights.'
  );

  d('dr-018', 'design', 'hard', '01 Kernels',
    'You must choose an attention backend for a server handling both 200k-token prefills and batch-256 short-context decode on H100. Walk through the decision.',
    'These are two different kernels and you should stop trying to pick one.\n\n**Prefill at 200k.** Compute bound, quadratic term dominates by a wide margin (200k is far past the ~30k crossover). Want FlashAttention-3 for the Hopper-specific throughput, plus chunking so the prefill does not monopolise iterations. Consider a sparse pattern (MInference) since dense 200k prefill is 40x the FLOPs of the linear term. Sequence parallelism if it will not fit in one GPU\u2019s memory during the forward pass.\n\n**Decode at batch 256, short context.** Memory bound. Parallelism is `256 * n_heads`, which already saturates the GPU, so Flash-Decoding\u2019s KV splitting buys nothing and its extra reduction kernel costs you. Want a paged decode kernel that reads the block table directly — FlashInfer or the engine\u2019s native paged kernel — and want it inside a CUDA graph.\n\n**The real answer** is an engine that dispatches per batch on the shape: FlashInfer exists precisely because a single block-sparse formulation can cover paged, ragged, and prefix-shared layouts, with a plan/run split that moves the layout decision off the hot path.\n\nWhat you should actually verify: that your engine is not using the prefill kernel for decode (check the trace), and that the KV layout is the one your decode kernel wants (HND vs NHD conventions differ and a transpose in the hot path is a silent 20% loss).'
  );

  /* ---------------- Module 02: KV cache ---------------- */
  d('dr-019', 'quant', 'medium', '02 KV cache',
    'Before PagedAttention, engines pre-allocated `max_model_len` per sequence. With `max_model_len=4096` and an actual output length distribution averaging 380 tokens on a 512-token prompt, what fraction of the KV cache is wasted?',
    '```\nreserved per seq = 4096 tokens\nused per seq     = 512 + 380 = 892 tokens\ninternal waste   = (4096 - 892) / 4096 = 78%\n```\n\nAbout **78%** of the KV cache is reserved and never touched. On the 52.9 GB budget from drill 06 that is 41 GB of an H100 doing nothing.\n\nThe vLLM paper measured 60-80% waste in exactly this way across real systems, split into three categories: *reserved* (allocated for future tokens of a live sequence), *internal fragmentation* (allocated beyond what the sequence will ever use), and *external fragmentation* (gaps between variable-size allocations).\n\nPaging attacks all three: blocks are allocated on demand (kills reserved waste), blocks are uniform in size (kills external fragmentation), and only the last block of a sequence is partly empty (bounds internal fragmentation to under one block per sequence, about 4% at block size 16).'
  );

  d('dr-020', 'concept', 'medium', '02 KV cache',
    'What does the block size in PagedAttention trade off? Why is 16 a common default rather than 1 or 256?',
    'Small blocks reduce internal fragmentation — the wasted tail is at most `block_size - 1` tokens per sequence — but increase the block table size, the number of indirections in the attention kernel, and the per-block bookkeeping cost. At block size 1 you have perfect memory efficiency and terrible kernel performance, because every token is a separate gather with no coalescing.\n\nLarge blocks give the kernel long contiguous runs to read, which is what HBM wants, but waste up to `block_size - 1` tokens per sequence and make prefix sharing coarser: two prompts that diverge mid-block cannot share that block at all.\n\n16 sits where the coalesced read is long enough to be efficient (16 tokens * 8 kv heads * 128 dims * 2 B = 32 KB per layer per block, comfortably above the memory transaction granularity) while the waste is about 4% at typical sequence lengths.\n\nThe prefix-caching consequence is the one people miss: hit rate is quantised to block boundaries. A shared prefix of 100 tokens with block size 16 yields 6 shared blocks, not 6.25.'
  );

  d('dr-021', 'quant', 'hard', '02 KV cache',
    'A chat service has a 1200-token system prompt shared by all requests, plus 300 tokens of user-specific content. Traffic is 50 requests/second on Llama 3.1 8B. Quantify what prefix caching saves in FLOPs and in TTFT, with block size 16.',
    '```\nshared blocks = floor(1200 / 16) = 75 blocks = 1200 tokens\nsaved FLOPs/req = 2 * 8.03e9 * 1200 = 19.3 TFLOP\nat 50 rps       = 963 TFLOP/s of demand removed\n```\n\nAn H100 at 45% MFU delivers 445 TFLOP/s, so prefix caching here removes **2.2 H100s worth** of prefill compute.\n\nTTFT: prefill of 1500 tokens takes `2*8.03e9*1500 / 445e12 = 54 ms`; of 300 tokens, `10.8 ms`. TTFT drops about **5x**, minus a cache lookup of well under a millisecond.\n\nMemory cost: `75 blocks * 16 tokens * 128 KiB = 150 MB`, stored once, shared by every request. That is 0.3% of the KV budget to remove two GPUs of work.\n\nThe trap: if any per-user token appears *before* the system prompt — a user ID, a timestamp, a randomised greeting — the hit rate is zero. Prefix order is a performance decision, not a formatting one.'
  );

  d('dr-022', 'concept', 'hard', '02 KV cache',
    'Compare hash-based prefix caching (vLLM) with a radix tree (SGLang). When does the radix tree actually win?',
    'Hash-based caching hashes each block along with the hash of all preceding blocks, giving a chain. A lookup walks the chain from token 0 and stops at the first miss. It is O(number of blocks), the data structure is a flat hash map, and eviction is straightforward LRU over blocks.\n\nA radix tree stores prefixes as a tree with path compression, so a lookup finds the longest matching prefix in one descent and *sharing is explicit*: sibling branches share their common ancestor nodes physically.\n\nThe hash chain already handles linear reuse perfectly well. The radix tree wins when the workload is **branching**:\n\n- Few-shot prompts where many requests share a long prefix then diverge — the tree makes eviction aware that the shared ancestor is hot even though each leaf is cold.\n- Multi-turn conversation where turn N+1 extends turn N — the tree naturally represents the extension.\n- Tree-of-thought, beam search, self-consistency sampling, and agent loops that fork from a common state.\n- Speculative decoding trees.\n\nWith LRU over a flat block map, a popular shared prefix can be evicted because each individual leaf looks cold. The tree makes the reference structure visible to the eviction policy, and SGLang\u2019s eviction explicitly protects nodes with live children.\n\nFor a plain single-turn chat workload the two perform about the same, and the hash approach is simpler.'
  );

  d('dr-023', 'concept', 'medium', '02 KV cache',
    'StreamingLLM keeps the first four tokens plus a sliding window and stays coherent to millions of tokens. Why do those four tokens matter, and why is this not a general long-context solution?',
    'Softmax must sum to 1. When a head has nothing it wants to attend to at a given position, it still has to put its probability mass somewhere. Models learn to dump it on the earliest tokens, which are visible to every position — these are **attention sinks**. Their content is irrelevant; their *position* is what matters.\n\nEvict them and the excess mass redistributes onto semantically meaningful tokens, corrupting the attention distribution. Perplexity explodes immediately. Keep four of them and a window of recent tokens, and the model stays fluent indefinitely.\n\nWhy it is not a general solution: StreamingLLM gives you unbounded *fluency*, not unbounded *context*. Information in the evicted middle is gone. The model cannot answer a question about token 500,000 when the window is 4096. It is the right tool for a never-ending streaming dialogue where only recent context matters, and the wrong tool for document QA.\n\nThe deeper lesson is that this behaviour was invisible until someone tried to evict, and it explains a whole class of otherwise mysterious KV-compression failures.'
  );

  d('dr-024', 'concept', 'hard', '02 KV cache',
    'KIVI quantises the key cache per channel and the value cache per token. Why the asymmetry?',
    'Because the outliers live in different places.\n\nThe key cache has **persistent outlier channels**: certain dimensions of the key vector have consistently large magnitude across all tokens, an artefact of the same emergent-outlier phenomenon LLM.int8() documented. If you quantise per token, each token\u2019s scale is dominated by those few channels and every other channel loses most of its resolution. Quantising per *channel* isolates the outlier dimensions into their own scale.\n\nThe value cache shows no such structured channel outliers; its magnitudes vary more by token. Per-token quantisation is therefore the right grouping, and it has a decisive systems advantage: a newly generated token can be quantised immediately and independently, whereas per-channel quantisation of the value cache would require re-deriving channel statistics as tokens stream in.\n\nThat systems asymmetry is why the algorithm is deployable. KVQuant reaches a similar conclusion by a different route and adds pre-RoPE key quantisation, since RoPE mixes channels and thereby smears the outlier structure that per-channel quantisation is trying to exploit.'
  );

  d('dr-025', 'quant', 'hard', '02 KV cache',
    'You are deciding whether to offload KV to host memory over PCIe Gen5 x16 (63 GB/s) or recompute the prefix. Llama 3.1 8B, prefix of 4000 tokens, prefill MFU 45%. Which wins, and at what point does the answer flip?',
    '```\nrecompute: 2 * 8.03e9 * 4000 / (989e12 * 0.45) = 144 ms\nfetch    : 4000 * 131,072 B / 63e9              = 8.3 ms\n```\n\nFetching wins by **17x**. Per token: 2.1 us to fetch versus 36 us to recompute.\n\nThe general condition is `kv_bytes_per_token / link_bw  <  2 * N_params / (peak_FLOPS * MFU)`.\n\nThe answer flips when:\n\n- **The link is contended.** Eight GPUs sharing host bandwidth cuts the effective rate to ~8 GB/s each, making the fetch 66 ms — still winning, but the margin is now 2x rather than 17x.\n- **The model is small.** A 1B model recomputes 8x faster while the KV per token only drops 4x.\n- **The KV cache is uncompressed and the model uses MHA.** 512 KiB/token quadruples the fetch cost.\n- **You are fetching from network storage** rather than host RAM. At 10 GB/s over RDMA the arithmetic is closer; at object-store latencies it is hopeless for interactive traffic.\n\nMooncake and LMCache exist because for realistic parameters the fetch usually wins, which makes a shared, tiered KV store worth building.'
  );

  d('dr-026', 'debug', 'hard', '02 KV cache',
    'Prefix cache hit rate is reported at 92% but TTFT has not improved at all. Give three plausible explanations.',
    '1. **The hits are on short prefixes.** Hit *rate* counts requests, not tokens. If 92% of requests hit on a 32-token prefix of a 2000-token prompt, you saved 1.6% of the prefill. Instrument saved *tokens*, not hit rate.\n\n2. **TTFT is dominated by queue time, not prefill.** If the server is at high utilisation, TTFT = queue + prefill and you only optimised the second term. Look at `vllm:time_in_queue_requests` separately. A cache hit that makes prefill 5x faster is invisible behind a 400 ms queue wait.\n\n3. **The hit is being counted but the blocks are being recomputed anyway.** Causes include: the cache is hit but immediately evicted under memory pressure before use; the blocks are on a different replica and the router is not cache-aware, so the "hit" is in a global counter but the local engine misses; or a sampling parameter (a LoRA adapter, a different `logits_processor`) invalidates reuse.\n\nA fourth possibility worth checking: you are measuring TTFT from a client that batches its own requests, so client-side queueing swamps the server-side improvement.'
  );

  d('dr-027', 'design', 'hard', '02 KV cache',
    'Design the KV storage tier for a service where 70% of requests reuse a conversation from within the last 10 minutes, average conversation state is 6000 tokens, at 200 requests/second on Llama 3.1 70B.',
    '**Working set.** 200 rps * 0.7 reuse * 600 s window = 84,000 conversations in the window, but only the distinct ones matter. Assume 20,000 distinct live conversations. At 320 KiB/token * 6000 tokens = 1.92 GB per conversation. Total **38 TB**. That does not fit in GPU memory by three orders of magnitude.\n\n**Tiering.**\n\n- *GPU HBM*: only actively decoding sequences. At batch 64 that is 123 GB — already needs TP=4 or more on H100 with the 141 GB of weights.\n- *Host DRAM*: the hot tail. 1-2 TB per node holds ~700 conversations. Fetch at 63 GB/s = 30 ms for a full 1.92 GB conversation, which is acceptable against a 70B prefill of 6000 tokens (about 850 ms at 45% MFU). Clear win.\n- *NVMe / RDMA-attached store*: the rest. At 7 GB/s, 274 ms to fetch — still 3x cheaper than recomputing.\n- *Recompute*: the fallback for a cold miss.\n\n**Quantise the KV to FP8 in the lower tiers.** Halves every number above and roughly halves fetch latency. Keep HBM in the native dtype for the active batch if quality is sensitive.\n\n**Routing is not optional here.** With a 38 TB working set spread across nodes, a conversation must go back to the node holding its KV, or the tiering buys nothing. Session-affinity routing with a consistent-hash fallback, and a global index of which node holds which conversation. This is essentially the Mooncake architecture, and the reason it is described as KV-cache-centric rather than compute-centric.\n\n**Eviction**: LRU with a TTL matched to the 10-minute window, plus explicit invalidation when a conversation ends.'
  );

  /* ---------------- Module 03: scheduling ---------------- */
  d('dr-028', 'quant', 'medium', '03 Scheduling',
    'Static batching, batch 8, on a workload where output lengths are 100, 120, 150, 200, 400, 500, 800 and 2000 tokens. Every sequence occupies a slot until the longest finishes. What is the utilisation, and what does continuous batching recover?',
    '```\ntokens actually generated = 100+120+150+200+400+500+800+2000 = 4270\nslot-steps consumed       = 8 * 2000 = 16,000\nutilisation               = 4270 / 16,000 = 27%\n```\n\nStatic batching wastes **73%** of the decode slots on padding, because seven sequences sit idle waiting for the 2000-token straggler.\n\nContinuous batching admits a new sequence the moment a slot frees, so utilisation approaches 100% subject only to KV availability and arrival rate. The theoretical speedup here is 3.7x; Anyscale measured 23x on a real workload because the real output-length distribution has a much heavier tail than this one and because static batching also loses on the prefill side.\n\nThe key insight from Orca is that the scheduling granularity must be the *iteration*, not the request. Selective batching is the second half: the attention operation cannot be batched across sequences with different lengths in the naive way, so Orca batches the linear layers and runs attention per sequence.'
  );

  d('dr-029', 'concept', 'hard', '03 Scheduling',
    'Explain a generation stall precisely, and show why chunked prefill fixes it without reducing total prefill throughput.',
    'A generation stall is what every decoding sequence experiences when an iteration is consumed by a long prefill.\n\nWithout chunking, the scheduler either runs the prefill alone or runs it alongside decodes in one iteration. A 8000-token prefill on Llama 3.1 8B takes about 290 ms at 45% MFU. Every sequence in the decode batch sees a 290 ms inter-token latency for that step, against a normal 6 ms. That is a 48x ITL spike, and it is exactly what shows up as a p99 disaster.\n\nChunked prefill splits the prefill into pieces sized to fit a per-iteration token budget `T`. With `T = 2048` and 64 decodes consuming 64 tokens of the budget, each iteration processes 1984 prefill tokens and the prefill spans 5 iterations. Each iteration now takes about 72 ms instead of one taking 290 ms — the *maximum* ITL drops by 4x and the distribution tightens.\n\nWhy total prefill throughput does not suffer: the chunks are still large enough to saturate the tensor cores. Prefill MFU is roughly flat above about 512 tokens per batch, so 1984 tokens is as efficient as 8000. You are re-slicing the same compute, not adding any.\n\nWhere it *does* cost you: TTFT for that request goes up, because its prefill now completes over 5 iterations rather than 1, and each iteration also carries decode work. Sarathi-Serve frames this as trading a small TTFT increase for a large TPOT tail improvement. If your product is TTFT-sensitive and TPOT-insensitive, chunk less aggressively.\n\nSecond-order effect: attention during a chunked prefill must attend to the KV of previous chunks, so later chunks are more expensive than earlier ones. The chunks are not equal in cost even though they are equal in token count.'
  );

  d('dr-030', 'quant', 'medium', '03 Scheduling',
    'You set `max_num_batched_tokens=8192` and `max_num_seqs=256`. Traffic is 2000-token prompts and 64 running decodes. How many iterations does one prefill take, and what happens if you drop the budget to 2048?',
    'Decodes claim one token each: 64 tokens of budget.\n\n```\nT=8192: prefill budget = 8128 -> 2000 tokens fit in 1 iteration\nT=2048: prefill budget = 1984 -> ceil(2000/1984) = 2 iterations\n```\n\nAt `T=8192` the prefill lands in one iteration of roughly `2*8.03e9*2064 / 445e12 = 74 ms`, and every decode sees a 74 ms ITL spike.\n\nAt `T=2048` each iteration is about 19 ms, so the ITL spike is 4x smaller, but TTFT for that request rises from 74 ms to about 38 ms of prefill spread over two iterations plus one extra scheduling round trip — in practice slightly worse TTFT, clearly better TPOT p99.\n\nThe knob to reason about is `max_num_batched_tokens` relative to your decode batch. If `max_num_batched_tokens` is only slightly larger than `max_num_seqs`, you have starved prefill entirely and TTFT will collapse under load.'
  );

  d('dr-031', 'concept', 'hard', '03 Scheduling',
    'When the KV cache fills, an engine can either swap a sequence to host memory or recompute it. Compare, and say which vLLM defaults to and why.',
    '**Swap**: copy the sequence\u2019s KV blocks to host memory, free the GPU blocks, and copy them back when rescheduled. Cost is `2 * S * kv_bytes_per_token / pcie_bw` for the round trip. For 2000 tokens on Llama 3.1 8B: `2 * 2000 * 131,072 / 63e9 = 8.3 ms`.\n\n**Recompute**: throw the KV away and re-run prefill on rescheduling. Cost is `2*N*S / (FLOPS*MFU)` = 72 ms for the same case.\n\nSwapping looks 9x cheaper, and for a single sequence it is. vLLM nonetheless defaults to **recompute**, for reasons that are systems rather than arithmetic:\n\n1. Swapping consumes PCIe bandwidth that is often already contended, and it does so *synchronously* on the critical path of an iteration that is also doing useful work.\n2. Swap requires a reserved host-side staging pool sized for the worst case; recompute requires nothing.\n3. Recomputation composes with prefix caching — the recomputed prefill may itself hit the cache, making it far cheaper than the worst case.\n4. Chunked prefill makes the recomputation splittable, so it does not reintroduce the generation stall that swapping avoids.\n\nThe real lesson is that preemption of either kind is a symptom. If `vllm:num_preemptions_total` is climbing under normal load, your `max_num_seqs` or `max_model_len` is set beyond what the KV budget supports and you should fix the admission limit rather than optimise the eviction path.'
  );

  d('dr-032', 'quant', 'hard', '03 Scheduling',
    'Your SLO is p95 TTFT under 500 ms. Measured service time per request is 900 ms and one replica sustains 12 requests/second at saturation. Using queueing theory, how many replicas do you need for 400 requests/second?',
    'Start with capacity: `400 / 12 = 33.3` replicas at 100% utilisation. That is the number that guarantees you miss the SLO.\n\nWith M/M/1 per replica, the waiting time is `W_q = rho / (mu * (1 - rho))` where `mu = 12/s`.\n\n```\nrho = 0.90 -> W_q = 0.90 / (12 * 0.10) = 750 ms   FAIL\nrho = 0.70 -> W_q = 0.70 / (12 * 0.30) = 194 ms   ok\nrho = 0.60 -> W_q = 0.60 / (12 * 0.40) = 125 ms   comfortable\n```\n\nAt rho = 0.70 you need `33.3 / 0.70 = 48 replicas`. At 0.60, **56 replicas**.\n\nCross-check with Little\u2019s Law: `L = lambda * W = 400 * 0.9 = 360` concurrent requests in the system. Divide by the per-replica concurrency your engine sustains at rho=0.7 to confirm the same answer.\n\nThree caveats that matter more than the arithmetic:\n\n1. M/M/1 assumes exponential service times. LLM service time is heavy-tailed because output length is, so the real p95 is *worse* than this model predicts. Treat the answer as a lower bound.\n2. TTFT is queue + prefill, so your 500 ms budget must also cover prefill. If prefill is 150 ms you only have 350 ms of queue budget, pushing rho down further.\n3. The 40% headroom is not waste, it is the SLO. Anyone who proposes running at 95% utilisation is proposing to delete your tail latency guarantee.'
  );

  d('dr-033', 'debug', 'hard', '03 Scheduling',
    'TPOT p50 is stable at 6 ms but p99 is 180 ms. Throughput and TTFT look fine. Walk through the diagnosis.',
    'A 30x gap between p50 and p99 ITL with healthy averages means something intermittently consumes a whole iteration. Candidates, in the order I would check them:\n\n1. **Unchunked prefills.** The signature is exact: p99 ITL approximately equals the prefill time of your longest prompts. Check whether chunked prefill is enabled and what `max_num_batched_tokens` is. This is the most common cause by a wide margin.\n\n2. **Preemption.** When the KV cache fills, sequences are evicted and later recomputed; the recomputation appears as a single enormous inter-token gap for that sequence. Check `vllm:num_preemptions_total` and `vllm:gpu_cache_usage_perc`. If cache usage is riding at 99%, this is it.\n\n3. **CUDA graph bucket padding.** If batch size crosses a capture bucket boundary, you pad up and occasionally recompile or fall out of the graph. Look for periodic rather than load-correlated spikes.\n\n4. **Detokenisation or streaming stalls.** A slow client, a blocking write on a backpressured HTTP stream, or per-token JSON serialisation on the server. These show up in the ITL as measured by the *client* but not in server-side scheduler metrics — comparing the two localises it immediately.\n\n5. **Other tenants or throttling.** Log SM clocks and power draw. A thermally throttled GPU produces exactly this pattern.\n\nThe method that resolves it fastest: emit per-iteration timing from the scheduler and correlate the slow iterations with what was scheduled in them. If the slow iterations all contain a prefill, you have your answer in one query.'
  );

  d('dr-034', 'concept', 'medium', '03 Scheduling',
    'Why is FCFS a poor scheduling policy for LLM serving, and why is shortest-job-first not available as a fix?',
    'FCFS suffers head-of-line blocking: a request that will generate 4000 tokens occupies a slot for 25 seconds, and every request behind it waits. Since output length varies by two orders of magnitude, the tail is dominated by unlucky queueing behind long jobs.\n\nSJF would fix it, but you cannot use it, because **you do not know the output length until the request finishes**. This is the structural difference between LLM serving and classical job scheduling, and it is why the literature is what it is.\n\nThe available responses:\n\n- **Multi-level feedback queue** (FastServe): start every request in the highest-priority queue and demote it as it consumes more service. This approximates SJF without prediction, at the cost of preemption machinery and the KV-state migration that preemption implies.\n- **Predict the length** with a small classifier on the prompt. Works better than it sounds for structured workloads, badly for open-ended chat.\n- **Let the client declare `max_tokens`** and schedule on that. Honest, cheap, and requires the client to be truthful — so pair it with enforcement.\n- **Fairness rather than efficiency** (VTC): stop trying to minimise mean latency and instead guarantee each tenant a share of service, counting tokens rather than requests since a request is not a unit of work.\n\nIn practice most production engines run FCFS with a priority tier and rely on chunked prefill plus admission control to bound the damage, because the preemption cost of anything smarter is real.'
  );

  d('dr-035', 'design', 'hard', '03 Scheduling',
    'Design admission control for a service with three tiers: interactive chat (p95 TTFT 300 ms), batch summarisation (no latency SLO), and an internal evaluation job (best effort). One GPU pool.',
    '**Principle: reject or defer early, never let the queue absorb overload.** A queue that grows without bound converts a capacity problem into a latency problem for everyone.\n\n**Admission.**\n\n- Maintain a token-budget accounting of in-flight work: `sum over running requests of (remaining prompt + expected output)`. Admit a new interactive request only if the projected KV occupancy stays under a threshold (say 85%) — this is what prevents preemption, which is what actually destroys p99.\n- Interactive: admit if the projected queue wait plus prefill is under 300 ms, otherwise shed with a 429 and a `Retry-After`. Mooncake\u2019s early-rejection design is exactly this, and its paper is candid that rejecting early beats admitting and missing.\n- Batch: admit only into leftover capacity. Deferrable by definition, so use a bounded queue with a long timeout.\n- Evaluation: run at the lowest priority with preemption enabled; it is the shock absorber.\n\n**Isolation.**\n\n- Separate `max_num_seqs` reservations per tier so batch cannot consume the whole KV budget. A simple reserved-share scheme is enough; VTC-style fair queueing if tenants within a tier need protection from each other.\n- Cap `max_tokens` per tier. Interactive gets 1024, batch gets 8192. Unbounded generation is an availability risk.\n\n**Scheduling.**\n\n- Chunked prefill with a token budget tuned so that batch prefills cannot stall interactive decodes. This is the single highest-leverage setting.\n- Priority-aware iteration scheduling: interactive decodes always claim budget first.\n\n**Feedback.**\n\n- Export per-tier TTFT, TPOT, queue time, admission rejections and preemptions. Alert on rejection rate and preemption rate, not on utilisation.\n- Autoscale on queue depth and SLO attainment, not on GPU utilisation, which for decode is nearly constant and nearly meaningless.\n\n**What I would explicitly not do**: give the tiers one shared FCFS queue and hope priority weighting sorts it out. Without KV-budget reservation the batch tier will fill the cache and the interactive tier will be preempted.'
  );

  /* ---------------- Module 04: measurement ---------------- */
  d('dr-036', 'concept', 'hard', '04 Measurement',
    'Explain why a closed-loop benchmark with fixed concurrency cannot find your capacity limit, and what to do instead.',
    'In a closed-loop test with N workers, a new request is issued only when a previous one completes. The offered load is therefore *self-limiting*: if the server slows down, the client slows down with it. The queue never grows, so you never observe queueing delay, and the measured latency is service time only.\n\nThe consequences are severe and specific:\n\n1. You cannot observe the capacity cliff. As you approach saturation the system degrades gracefully in the test and catastrophically in production.\n2. p99 is understated, often by an order of magnitude, because the tail in production comes from queueing behind bursts that the closed-loop test cannot produce.\n3. Throughput looks like a monotone function of concurrency, so you conclude "more concurrency is better" and set `max_num_seqs` far too high.\n\n**Instead: open-loop, rate-based load.** Issue requests according to an arrival process (Poisson is a reasonable default; a replayed production trace is better) independent of server response. Sweep the rate. Plot latency percentiles against rate. The capacity limit is where p95 crosses your SLO — that is the number you deploy against.\n\nThis is what GuideLLM\u2019s rate sweep and constraint-based modes are for, and why the MLPerf "Server" scenario is defined with a Poisson arrival process rather than a fixed concurrency.\n\nUse closed-loop tests only to measure *maximum* offline throughput, where latency genuinely does not matter.'
  );

  d('dr-037', 'quant', 'medium', '04 Measurement',
    'A summarisation benchmark reports 4200 tokens/second. Prompts average 4000 tokens, outputs average 100. Is the service fast? What is the number that matters?',
    'You cannot tell, because 4200 tok/s is almost certainly *total* throughput.\n\n```\nratio in:out = 4000:100 = 40:1\ntotal tok/s  = 4200\noutput tok/s = 4200 * 100/4100 = 102 tok/s\n```\n\nSo the service generates about **102 output tokens per second** in aggregate — that is 1.02 requests per second. If a user is waiting on a 100-token summary, they wait 1 second for generation plus whatever prefill and queueing cost.\n\nThe rule: state which throughput you mean, every time. Output token throughput is what determines how many concurrent users you can serve at a given TPOT. Total token throughput tells you about hardware utilisation. They differ by the prompt:completion ratio, which in production workloads ranges from 1:10 (reasoning) to 40:1 (summarisation).\n\nA related trap: comparing tokens/second across models with different tokenizers. A 30% more efficient tokenizer produces 30% fewer tokens for the same text and will look 30% slower on this metric while being faster in wall clock.'
  );

  d('dr-038', 'debug', 'medium', '04 Measurement',
    'Your benchmark shows 40x better TTFT than the same config showed last week. Nothing changed in the engine. What do you check first?',
    'Prefix caching, and whether your synthetic prompts share a prefix.\n\nA 40x TTFT improvement is not an optimisation, it is a cache hit. If your load generator builds prompts from a template with a small random suffix, every request after the first hits the cache for nearly the whole prompt and you are measuring the cache lookup, not the model.\n\nCheck, in order:\n\n1. `vllm:prefix_cache_hit_rate` or the equivalent. If it is near 1.0, that is your answer.\n2. Whether the dataset changed. A switch from a random-token dataset to ShareGPT, or a change in how the harness seeds prompts, does this.\n3. Whether warm-up is now included. If last week\u2019s run included cold-start compilation in the measurement window and this week\u2019s does not, TTFT for the first requests changes enormously.\n\nThe methodological fix: decide explicitly whether you are benchmarking with caching on or off, state it in the result, and if it is on, use a prompt distribution whose prefix-sharing structure matches production. Reporting a cached TTFT as if it were an engine improvement is the most common way benchmark results become dishonest without anyone intending it.'
  );

  d('dr-039', 'concept', 'medium', '04 Measurement',
    'What must be identical between two engines before a throughput comparison means anything? List the ones people usually forget.',
    'The obvious ones: model, weight dtype, hardware, tensor parallel degree, and the prompt/output length distribution.\n\nThe ones people forget:\n\n- **KV cache dtype.** FP8 KV doubles capacity and changes throughput materially. Frequently defaulted differently between engines.\n- **`max_num_seqs` / `max_batch_size`.** Defaults differ by 4x between engines. This alone can produce the entire reported difference.\n- **`max_num_batched_tokens`** and whether chunked prefill is on.\n- **`gpu_memory_utilization`.** 0.90 versus 0.95 is a 25% difference in KV budget.\n- **`max_model_len`.** Reducing it increases the number of sequences that fit, and some engines default to the model\u2019s full context while others cap it.\n- **Prefix caching on or off.**\n- **CUDA graphs / eager mode.** vLLM\u2019s `enforce_eager` costs 30-50% and is a common accidental setting in scripts.\n- **Attention backend.** FlashInfer versus FlashAttention versus the native paged kernel differ by 10-20%.\n- **Sampling parameters.** Greedy versus top-p with a large vocabulary changes sampler cost; guided decoding changes it a lot.\n- **Whether the client is the bottleneck.** Check client CPU.\n- **Whether the run is long enough** to reach steady state, and whether warm-up was discarded.\n\nPublish the full configuration alongside the number, or the number is an opinion.'
  );

  d('dr-040', 'quant', 'hard', '04 Measurement',
    'You measure a decode step at 8.1 ms. Your arithmetic predicted 4.8 ms. Enumerate where the 3.3 ms could be and how you would attribute it.',
    'Predicted 4.8 ms assumed 100% of HBM bandwidth and pure weight traffic. The gap decomposes into four buckets, and a timeline profile attributes them directly:\n\n1. **Bandwidth efficiency.** A good kernel achieves 70-90% of peak. At 80% the prediction becomes 6.0 ms. This accounts for 1.2 ms and is not recoverable without kernel work.\n\n2. **KV traffic you ignored.** At batch 32 and 4096 context, KV bytes are `32 * 4096 * 131,072 = 17.2 GB` per step against 16.06 GB of weights — you underestimated bytes by more than 2x. Recompute before blaming anything else. This is the most common cause of exactly this discrepancy.\n\n3. **Launch and host overhead.** 380 kernels at 6 us is 2.3 ms if CUDA graphs are off. Check `enforce_eager`. In the trace this appears as gaps between kernels.\n\n4. **Non-GEMM kernels.** LayerNorm/RMSNorm, RoPE, the residual adds, and the sampler are all memory bound and all read and write activations. They are usually 10-20% of decode step time and are invisible in a weights-only model.\n\nAttribution method: take an Nsight Systems trace of ten steady-state steps. Sum kernel time by category. The difference between wall time and summed kernel time is host overhead. Within kernel time, compare each kernel\u2019s achieved bandwidth against peak using Nsight Compute on the top three by duration.'
  );

  d('dr-041', 'concept', 'hard', '04 Measurement',
    'You quantised a model to INT4 and perplexity moved from 6.14 to 6.21. Is it safe to ship?',
    'No. Perplexity is a smoke test, not a gate.\n\nPerplexity averages log-likelihood over all tokens, and the overwhelming majority of tokens in any corpus are easy and highly predictable — function words, common continuations, formatting. Quantisation error concentrates on the rare, high-information tokens that determine whether a multi-step reasoning chain succeeds. A 1% perplexity delta is entirely compatible with a 5-point drop on GSM8K.\n\nWhat you should run before shipping:\n\n- **Task benchmarks** relevant to your product, through lm-evaluation-harness: GSM8K or MATH for arithmetic reasoning, MMLU for knowledge, HumanEval for code, IFEval for instruction following.\n- **A long-context probe** if you touched the KV cache at all: RULER, not just needle-in-a-haystack, which almost everything passes.\n- **Your own production evaluation set**, which is the only one that measures what you actually sell.\n- **A generation-length check.** Quantised models sometimes become more repetitive or more verbose; both change your cost model.\n\nAnd measure the actual speedup on your batch size before spending the quality budget at all. If you are compute bound at batch 256, W4A16 buys you capacity but no speed, and you have paid an accuracy cost for nothing.'
  );

  d('dr-042', 'design', 'medium', '04 Measurement',
    'Design the observability for an LLM serving fleet: what do you export, what do you alert on, and what do you deliberately not alert on?',
    '**Export, per replica.**\n\n- Latency: TTFT, TPOT and E2E as *histograms*, not averages. You need p50/p95/p99 and you cannot compute percentiles from means.\n- Decomposed TTFT: queue time and prefill time separately. Without this you cannot tell a capacity problem from a compute problem.\n- Scheduler state: running sequences, waiting sequences, KV cache usage percent, preemption count, and the per-iteration token budget consumed.\n- Cache: prefix cache hit rate *and* prefix cache hit tokens.\n- Throughput: prompt tokens/s and generation tokens/s, separately.\n- Hardware: SM clock, memory clock, power, temperature, ECC errors.\n- Errors: OOM, CUDA errors, timeouts, 429s.\n\n**Alert on.**\n\n- SLO attainment: fraction of requests meeting the TTFT and TPOT targets, over a rolling window. This is the alert that matters.\n- Queue depth growth rate — the leading indicator of the capacity cliff.\n- Preemption rate above zero under normal load.\n- Rejection rate.\n- Error rate and any nonzero ECC or XID event.\n\n**Do not alert on.**\n\n- GPU utilisation. It is near-constant during decode and tells you nothing about health.\n- Mean latency. It hides exactly the behaviour that hurts.\n- Throughput in isolation. Throughput falling because traffic fell is not an incident.\n- Cache hit rate. It is workload-dependent and a drop may be entirely legitimate.\n\n**Also capture**: a sampled request trace with per-phase timings, so that when p99 moves you can look at an actual slow request rather than reasoning from aggregates.'
  );

  /* ---------------- Module 05: quantization ---------------- */
  d('dr-043', 'quant', 'medium', '05 Quantization',
    'Llama 3.1 70B on one H100 80GB. BF16 weights are 141 GB, so it does not fit. Show what INT4 buys you and what the decode step time becomes.',
    '```\nBF16 weights : 70.6e9 * 2 = 141 GB          -> does not fit\nINT4 weights : 70.6e9 * 0.5 = 35.3 GB       -> fits\ngroup-wise scales (g=128, FP16): 70.6e9/128*2 = 1.1 GB\ntotal weight footprint approx 36.4 GB\n```\n\nFree memory for KV at `gpu_memory_utilization=0.90`:\n\n```\n0.90*80 - 36.4 - 2 (activations/workspace) = 33.6 GB\nKV/token = 2*80*8*128*2 = 327,680 B = 320 KiB\ntokens   = 33.6e9 / 327,680 = 102,500 tokens\n```\n\nDecode step time, batch 1, weight traffic only:\n\n```\nBF16 hypothetical (2 GPUs) : 141e9 / 3.35e12 = 42 ms\nINT4 (1 GPU)               : 36.4e9 / 3.35e12 = 10.9 ms -> 92 tok/s\n```\n\nSo INT4 turns a two-GPU deployment into a one-GPU deployment and roughly quadruples single-stream speed. This is the case where weight-only quantisation is unambiguously correct: you are memory bound, you are memory *capacity* constrained, and the arithmetic intensity is nowhere near the ridge point.\n\nThe caveat is the dequantisation cost. W4A16 kernels must unpack INT4 to BF16 before the tensor-core GEMM, which adds ALU work. Marlin and Machete exist because the naive kernel loses much of the theoretical gain; a bad kernel can deliver 1.5x where the arithmetic promised 4x.'
  );

  d('dr-044', 'concept', 'hard', '05 Quantization',
    'You apply W4A16 to a model serving batch 256 with 8000-token contexts and observe no speedup. Explain.',
    'Because you are not memory bound on weights at that operating point, so shrinking the weights does not shrink the critical path.\n\nCompute the arithmetic intensity. At batch 256, the linear layers see a GEMM with M=256, which is well past the ridge point (B_crit is 295 on H100 for BF16, and the effective threshold for the GEMM to be compute bound is far lower once you account for the fact that weights are amortised across 256 rows). The linear layers are compute bound; INT4 weights reduce weight *bytes* but the kernel still does BF16 tensor-core math after dequantisation, so the FLOPs are unchanged — and you have *added* the unpack work.\n\nSecond, at 8000-token context and batch 256, KV traffic is `256 * 8000 * kv_bytes` which dwarfs the weights. For Llama 3.1 8B: `256*8000*131,072 = 268 GB` per decode step versus 16 GB of weights. Weights are 6% of the byte traffic. Halving 6% of the traffic cannot produce a visible speedup even in the memory-bound limit.\n\n**What to quantise instead in this regime:**\n\n- **KV cache to FP8.** That is the 268 GB. Halving it nearly halves decode step time and doubles the sequences you can hold.\n- **Activations to FP8** (W8A8), which halves the actual tensor-core work — this is the only quantisation that helps a compute-bound GEMM, because FP8 tensor cores are genuinely 2x BF16.\n\nThe general rule: **quantise the thing that dominates your byte or FLOP budget at your batch size.** Weight-only quantisation is a small-batch and capacity-constrained technique. W8A8/FP8 is the large-batch technique. Reciting "INT4 is faster" without computing the intensity is the mistake.'
  );

  d('dr-045', 'concept', 'hard', '05 Quantization',
    'Why do activations need per-token dynamic scaling while weights can use static per-channel scaling, and what does SmoothQuant do about it?',
    'Weights are fixed at serving time. You can compute their exact range offline, choose per-output-channel scales that are optimal for that tensor, and pay nothing at runtime. Their distribution is also comparatively benign — roughly Gaussian, moderate dynamic range.\n\nActivations are input-dependent, so any static scale must cover the worst case you might ever see, and they contain **systematic outliers**: in transformer models above roughly 6.7B parameters, a small number of feature dimensions carry values 20-100x larger than the rest, and they appear in the *same* channels across all tokens. LLM.int8() documented this and it is the central difficulty of activation quantisation. With INT8 and a per-tensor scale set by an outlier of magnitude 100, an ordinary value of 0.5 quantises to zero.\n\nPer-token dynamic scaling helps because it adapts to each token\u2019s actual range, but it does not solve the problem: the outlier is *within* the token, in specific channels, so the token\u2019s scale is still dominated by it.\n\n**SmoothQuant\u2019s move**: migrate the difficulty from activations to weights by a mathematically equivalent rescaling. For `Y = X W`, insert a diagonal `s`:\n\n```\nY = (X diag(s)^-1) (diag(s) W)\ns_j = max|X_j|^alpha / max|W_j|^(1-alpha),  alpha ~ 0.5\n```\n\nThe activation channel with the outlier is divided down; the corresponding weight column is scaled up. Weights tolerate this because their range was not the binding constraint. `diag(s)^-1` folds into the preceding LayerNorm, so it is free at runtime. The result is that both tensors become quantisable to INT8 with per-tensor or per-token scales, and W8A8 becomes viable — which matters because W8A8 is the variant that actually speeds up compute-bound GEMMs.\n\nAWQ attacks the same problem from the other side: identify the ~1% of weight channels that are salient *because of the activation magnitudes they multiply*, and scale them to protect them, using activation statistics rather than weight magnitude to decide what matters.'
  );

  d('dr-046', 'quant', 'medium', '05 Quantization',
    'Compare FP8 E4M3 and INT8 for the same 8 bits. Why does FP8 usually win for activations and INT8 sometimes win for weights?',
    '```\nINT8      : uniform steps, range +-127, ~2.4 decimal digits, step = range/256\nFP8 E4M3  : 4 exponent + 3 mantissa bits, max 448, ~2.4 significant bits\n            but the step size scales with magnitude\n```\n\nThe difference is not precision, it is **how the precision is distributed**. INT8 spends its 256 levels uniformly across the range, so if the range is set by an outlier of 100, the resolution near zero is 0.78 — catastrophic when most values are near zero. FP8 spends levels logarithmically: it has fine resolution near zero and coarse resolution near the outlier, which is exactly the right allocation for a heavy-tailed distribution.\n\nActivations are heavy-tailed, so FP8 wins. It also wins operationally: on Hopper and later, FP8 tensor cores run at 2x BF16, and the scaling can be per-tensor rather than requiring the elaborate outlier machinery.\n\nWeights are closer to Gaussian and bounded, so the uniform allocation is not wasteful, and INT8 (or INT4) with per-channel or per-group scales gives you more effective levels within the actual range. That is why weight-only quantisation research is dominated by integer formats — GPTQ, AWQ, and the group-wise INT4 kernels — while activation and KV quantisation moved to FP8.\n\nOne more practical point: FP8 E5M2 exists too, with more exponent and less mantissa. It is used for gradients in training where dynamic range matters more than precision. For inference activations E4M3 is the standard choice.'
  );

  d('dr-047', 'quant', 'hard', '05 Quantization',
    'You quantise the KV cache to FP8 on Llama 3.1 8B, batch 64, 8192 context. Quantify the two benefits, then say what you must test.',
    '```\nBF16 KV : 2*32*8*128*2 = 131,072 B/token = 128 KiB\nFP8 KV  : 2*32*8*128*1 =  65,536 B/token =  64 KiB\n\nCapacity at 57 GB of free KV budget:\n  BF16 : 57e9/131,072 = 435,000 tokens = 53 sequences at 8192\n  FP8  : 57e9/ 65,536 = 870,000 tokens = 106 sequences at 8192\n\nDecode step bytes at batch 64, 8192 ctx:\n  weights            = 16.06 GB (unchanged)\n  KV BF16 = 64*8192*131,072 = 68.7 GB  -> total 84.8 GB -> 25.3 ms\n  KV FP8  = 64*8192* 65,536 = 34.4 GB  -> total 50.4 GB -> 15.0 ms\n```\n\nSo: **2x the concurrent sequences and a 1.7x faster decode step.** At this operating point KV quantisation is worth far more than weight quantisation, which would only touch the 16 GB.\n\n**What you must test**, because KV quantisation degrades differently from weight quantisation:\n\n1. **Long-context retrieval.** Error in the KV cache accumulates over the sequence and hits attention over distant tokens hardest. Run RULER at your maximum supported length, not just needle-in-a-haystack.\n2. **Multi-turn coherence.** The KV for turn 1 is quantised and then attended to for the rest of the conversation.\n3. **Whether you quantised K and V with the same scheme.** The K cache is significantly more sensitive than V, because K participates in the softmax where errors are amplified exponentially. KIVI quantises K per-channel and V per-token for exactly this reason. If your engine offers separate K and V dtypes, consider FP8 V with BF16 K as an intermediate step.\n4. **Scale calibration.** Per-tensor static scales for KV are risky; prefer per-head or per-token dynamic scaling if the kernel supports it.'
  );

  d('dr-048', 'concept', 'medium', '05 Quantization',
    'GPTQ and AWQ both produce INT4 weights. What is actually different, and when would you pick each?',
    '**GPTQ** is second-order error compensation. It quantises weights column by column, and after quantising each column it updates the remaining unquantised columns to compensate for the error introduced, using the inverse Hessian of the layer\u2019s reconstruction objective estimated from calibration activations. It is a per-layer optimal-brain-surgeon procedure made tractable by processing in a fixed order with a Cholesky reformulation.\n\n**AWQ** is activation-aware scaling. It observes that not all weights matter equally, and that the ones that matter are identified by the *magnitude of the activations they multiply*, not by their own magnitude. It searches for a per-channel scale that protects the ~1% salient channels, then quantises everything uniformly with round-to-nearest. No backpropagation, no Hessian, no reconstruction — it is a search over one scalar per channel per layer.\n\nPractical differences:\n\n- **Calibration sensitivity.** AWQ is markedly more robust to calibration set choice; GPTQ can overfit to the calibration distribution, which shows up as degradation on out-of-domain prompts.\n- **Speed of quantisation.** AWQ is faster to run and simpler to reproduce.\n- **Accuracy.** Comparable on most benchmarks; GPTQ sometimes edges ahead at very low bit widths or with careful group sizes, AWQ tends to be better at generalisation and on instruction-tuned models.\n- **Kernel support.** Both have good kernels; Marlin and Machete serve GPTQ-style layouts, and AWQ has its own. Check what your engine actually accelerates before choosing — the format your kernel is fastest at matters more than a 0.1 perplexity difference.\n\nI would default to **AWQ** for instruction-tuned production models because of the calibration robustness, and reach for GPTQ when I have a well-matched calibration set and want to push to 3 bits.'
  );

  d('dr-049', 'debug', 'hard', '05 Quantization',
    'An FP8 model passes all offline evals but produces occasional NaNs in production. Where do you look?',
    'FP8 E4M3 has a maximum representable value of 448. NaN in an FP8 pipeline nearly always means an overflow that saturated to inf and then propagated through a subtraction or a division.\n\nCheck, in order:\n\n1. **Scale calibration coverage.** If your scales were calibrated on a dataset whose activation ranges are narrower than production, a production prompt with larger activations overflows. This is the single most common cause. Look for prompts with unusual structure — long repeated tokens, very high-entropy input, non-English scripts, or code with long identifier runs.\n\n2. **Static versus dynamic scaling.** Static per-tensor scales are fast and fragile. If the failure correlates with specific inputs, move the offending layers to dynamic per-token scaling and re-measure the cost.\n\n3. **The attention softmax.** If you quantised the attention inputs, the pre-softmax scores can be large; a softmax without a max subtraction in the accumulator, or with the max computed in FP8, overflows. Verify the accumulation dtype is FP32 throughout the softmax.\n\n4. **Layers that should never have been quantised.** The LM head, the embedding, and the first and last transformer blocks are conventionally excluded. Check the exclusion list in your quantisation config actually took effect — a silent name mismatch in the ignore pattern is common.\n\n5. **KV cache scales.** If K is quantised with a static scale and a long context produces larger keys than calibration saw, you get inf in the score computation.\n\n**Method**: reproduce with the offending prompt, hook every layer to record the max absolute value of its input and output, and find the first layer whose output exceeds 448 before scaling. Then decide whether to exclude it, rescale it, or move it to dynamic scaling. Also add a production guard: a NaN check on logits with a fallback to a BF16 replica is cheap insurance.'
  );

  d('dr-050', 'design', 'hard', '05 Quantization',
    'You must cut serving cost for a 70B chat model by 50% with under 1 point of degradation on your eval suite. Design the plan.',
    '**Step 0: measure before choosing.** Establish the operating point — batch size, context distribution, current cost per million output tokens, and where the bytes and FLOPs go. Every subsequent decision depends on whether you are weight bound, KV bound, or compute bound.\n\n**Step 1: the highest-leverage change is usually FP8 W8A8 on Hopper.** Weights 141 -> 70 GB, and the GEMMs run on FP8 tensor cores at 2x. This is a genuine speedup in both the memory-bound and compute-bound regimes, and FP8 with per-tensor or per-channel scales is the best-understood production quantisation there is. Expect well under 1 point on most suites. This alone plausibly takes you from 4 GPUs to 2.\n\n**Step 2: FP8 KV cache.** At a chat workload with 4-8k contexts and a large batch, KV is a large share of the byte traffic and the binding constraint on batch size. Halving it raises the achievable batch, which raises throughput per GPU superlinearly in the memory-bound regime.\n\n**Step 3: measure again.** After 1 and 2, recompute the operating point. It is very likely you are now compute bound at your batch size, in which case further weight compression buys nothing and you should stop.\n\n**Step 4, only if still short: INT4 weight-only (AWQ)** for the capacity benefit, accepting the dequantisation overhead, and re-validate carefully. This is where the accuracy budget gets spent.\n\n**Orthogonal levers I would apply before INT4**, because they cost no accuracy at all:\n\n- Prefix caching, if the workload has a shared system prompt. In chat this is often a 30-50% prefill reduction outright.\n- Chunked prefill and a correctly tuned `max_num_seqs`, which frequently recovers 20-30% of throughput that was being lost to misconfiguration.\n- Speculative decoding if the batch is small and latency is the constraint.\n\n**Validation gate**: GSM8K, MMLU, IFEval, HumanEval, a RULER run at max context, and the internal eval suite, all with confidence intervals, plus a side-by-side human preference check on 200 production prompts. Ship behind a flag with a BF16 fallback and compare live quality metrics for a week.\n\n**What I would refuse to do**: ship on perplexity alone, or quantise and evaluate in the same week without a canary.'
  );

  /* ---------------- Module 06: decoding ---------------- */
  d('dr-051', 'quant', 'hard', '06 Decoding',
    'Draft model proposes k=4 tokens, acceptance rate alpha=0.7, draft costs 15% of a target step. Compute the expected speedup, and find the optimal k.',
    'Expected accepted tokens per verification round, for the standard rejection scheme where the first rejection stops the run and a bonus token comes from the corrected distribution:\n\n```\nE[accepted] = (1 - alpha^(k+1)) / (1 - alpha)\n            = (1 - 0.7^5) / 0.3 = (1 - 0.168) / 0.3 = 2.77 tokens\n```\n\nCost per round, in units of one target step:\n\n```\ncost = k * c_draft + 1 = 4*0.15 + 1 = 1.60\nspeedup = 2.77 / 1.60 = 1.73x\n```\n\nSweeping k:\n\n```\nk=1: E=1.70, cost=1.15 -> 1.48x\nk=2: E=2.19, cost=1.30 -> 1.68x\nk=3: E=2.53, cost=1.45 -> 1.75x\nk=4: E=2.77, cost=1.60 -> 1.73x\nk=5: E=2.94, cost=1.75 -> 1.68x\nk=6: E=3.06, cost=1.90 -> 1.61x\n```\n\n**Optimal k=3 at 1.75x.** The shape is the important part: E[accepted] saturates at `1/(1-alpha) = 3.33` while cost grows linearly in k, so there is always an interior optimum and overshooting k is worse than undershooting.\n\nSensitivity: at alpha=0.5 the ceiling is 2.0 accepted tokens and the best you can do is about 1.35x. At alpha=0.9 the ceiling is 10 and larger k pays off — k=8 gives 2.9x. **Acceptance rate is the whole game**, and it is a property of how well the draft matches the target on *your* distribution, which is why measuring alpha on production traffic is the first thing to do.'
  );

  d('dr-052', 'concept', 'hard', '06 Decoding',
    'Prove that speculative decoding preserves the target distribution exactly, and explain what breaks the guarantee in practice.',
    'Let `p` be the target distribution and `q` the draft, both at the same position. The draft samples `x ~ q`. Accept with probability `min(1, p(x)/q(x))`. On rejection, sample from the residual `p\'(x) = norm(max(0, p(x) - q(x)))`.\n\nThe probability the algorithm outputs `x`:\n\n```\nP(output x) = q(x) * min(1, p(x)/q(x))  +  P(reject) * p\'(x)\n```\n\nFirst term: `q(x)*min(1, p(x)/q(x)) = min(q(x), p(x))`.\n\nRejection probability: `P(reject) = 1 - sum_x min(q(x), p(x)) = sum_x max(0, p(x) - q(x))`, which is exactly the normalising constant of `p\'`. So the second term is `sum_x max(0,p(x)-q(x)) * max(0, p(x)-q(x))/sum(...) = max(0, p(x) - q(x))`.\n\nAdding: `min(p,q) + max(0, p-q) = p(x)`. Exactly the target distribution, for every `x`. The argument extends to a run of k tokens by induction, since each position is verified against the target conditional given the accepted prefix.\n\n**What breaks it in practice:**\n\n1. **Numerical non-determinism.** The target logits computed in the batched verification pass are not bitwise identical to the logits the same model would produce autoregressively, because the GEMM shapes and reduction orders differ. The distribution is the same in exact arithmetic; in floating point it is not, so outputs can differ from a non-speculative run. This is a reproducibility issue, not a correctness one, but it surprises people.\n\n2. **Lossy variants.** Anything that accepts a token whose `p(x)/q(x)` ratio is below the threshold — typical-acceptance, relaxed thresholds, "lenient" modes — trades the guarantee for a higher acceptance rate. They are often fine, but they are no longer distribution-preserving and must be evaluated as a model change.\n\n3. **Mismatched sampling parameters.** If the draft samples at a different temperature or with different top-p than the target verification assumes, the ratio is computed against the wrong `q` and the correction is wrong.\n\n4. **Greedy decoding** is the degenerate case: acceptance becomes "did the draft pick the argmax", and the output is bitwise the target\u2019s greedy output up to the numerics point above.'
  );

  d('dr-053', 'concept', 'medium', '06 Decoding',
    'Why does speculative decoding stop helping at large batch sizes?',
    'Because it trades compute for latency, and at large batch you have no spare compute to trade.\n\nAt batch 1, the decode step is deeply memory bound: arithmetic intensity is about 2 FLOP/byte against a ridge point of 295. The GPU is idle 99% of the time in FLOP terms. Verifying 4 tokens instead of 1 costs essentially nothing extra in wall clock because you are still just streaming weights — you have converted an idle tensor core into free work.\n\nAs batch grows, the arithmetic intensity grows with it. Around batch 128-256 on H100 you approach the ridge and the GEMMs become compute bound. Now verifying k tokens for B sequences means a GEMM with M = B*k instead of M = B, and that is genuinely `k` times more FLOPs on a unit that is already saturated. The verification step takes k times longer, and the speedup collapses to `E[accepted]/k <= 1` — worse than not speculating, once you add the draft cost.\n\nThe practical rule: speculative decoding is a **low-batch, latency-optimising** technique. Enable it when your batch is small (interactive single-user, low-traffic endpoints, agent loops with one request in flight) and disable it above a threshold you determine empirically — usually somewhere between batch 16 and 64 depending on model and hardware.\n\nA good production design makes this dynamic: the scheduler enables speculation when the running batch is below the threshold and turns it off above it. vLLM and TensorRT-LLM both expose variants of this.'
  );

  d('dr-054', 'concept', 'hard', '06 Decoding',
    'Compare a separate draft model, Medusa heads, EAGLE, and n-gram/prompt lookup. What determines the acceptance rate in each?',
    '**Separate draft model** (e.g. Llama 3.2 1B drafting for 70B). Acceptance depends on how well the small model matches the large one\u2019s distribution — typically 0.6-0.8 for same-family models, much lower across families. Costs a second set of weights in memory and a second forward pass. Simple, well understood, and the draft can be independently improved by fine-tuning it on the target\u2019s outputs.\n\n**Medusa**: extra decoding heads bolted onto the target\u2019s final hidden state, each predicting position t+1, t+2, ... in parallel. No separate model, tiny memory cost. But the heads condition only on the *current* hidden state, not on the tokens the earlier heads proposed, so accuracy degrades quickly with depth. Mitigated with tree attention over multiple candidates per head. Typical 2-3x.\n\n**EAGLE**: the key insight is to autoregress at the *feature* level rather than the token level. A single lightweight layer takes the target\u2019s hidden states plus the token embeddings of the already-drafted tokens and predicts the next hidden state, which is then decoded to a token. Because it is genuinely autoregressive on features, and because it uses the target\u2019s own representations, acceptance is much higher than Medusa at the same depth — EAGLE-2 and EAGLE-3 report 3-4x+ and dynamic draft-tree construction. This is the current strong default.\n\n**N-gram / prompt lookup**: no model at all. Search the prompt and the generated text so far for a match to the last few tokens, and propose the continuation that followed. Acceptance is near zero on open-ended generation and very high on tasks with heavy copying — summarisation, code editing, RAG with quotation, retrieval-augmented answers. Free to run. Enable it for those workloads; it is pure profit.\n\n**Selection logic**: if your workload copies from the input, use prompt lookup first because it costs nothing. If you need general speedup and can afford the engineering, EAGLE. If you need something today with minimal risk and have a small same-family model, use it as a draft.'
  );

  d('dr-055', 'quant', 'medium', '06 Decoding',
    'Prompt-lookup decoding on a summarisation workload: 60% of generated tokens are copied verbatim from the source, and when a match is found you propose 10 tokens with 85% of them accepted. Estimate the speedup.',
    'Model it as a mixture. Let `f = 0.6` be the fraction of steps where the lookup finds a usable match.\n\n```\nmatch found (60% of rounds):\n  proposed 10, accepted ~ 0.85*10 = 8.5 tokens, cost = 1 target step\n  (lookup itself is a string search, effectively free)\nno match (40% of rounds):\n  1 token, cost = 1 target step\n\nE[tokens per target step] = 0.6*8.5 + 0.4*1 = 5.1 + 0.4 = 5.5\n```\n\nNaively 5.5x. Two corrections bring it down:\n\n1. The verification GEMM with 10 extra positions is not free at moderate batch. At batch 1 it is nearly free; at batch 32 it costs maybe 20-30% more per step. Divide by 1.25 -> 4.4x.\n2. Acceptance within a proposed run is sequential: the run stops at the first rejection. "85% accepted" as an independent per-token rate gives `E = (1-0.85^11)/0.15 = 5.9`, not 8.5. Redo with that: `0.6*5.9 + 0.4*1 = 3.9`, then /1.25 -> **about 3.1x**.\n\nThat second correction is the one people get wrong, and it is the same correction as in the draft-model case: acceptance compounds geometrically, it does not average.\n\nEven 3x for zero memory cost and no extra model is an outstanding trade, which is why prompt lookup should be the default for any copy-heavy workload.'
  );

  d('dr-056', 'concept', 'medium', '06 Decoding',
    'How does constrained/structured decoding work, and what is the performance cost?',
    'The mechanism: at each decoding step, compute a mask over the vocabulary that marks which tokens can legally extend the output under the grammar, and set the logits of illegal tokens to `-inf` before sampling. The grammar is typically a JSON schema compiled to a regular expression or a context-free grammar, tracked by a finite automaton or a pushdown automaton over the token stream.\n\nThe hard part is that grammars are defined over *characters* while sampling is over *tokens*, and the tokenizer\u2019s merges mean one token spans many characters. You need, for each automaton state, the set of tokens whose character expansion keeps you in a valid state. Outlines\u2019 contribution was to precompute this index offline: for each FSM state, a map to allowed token IDs, making the per-step cost an O(1) lookup instead of a scan over a 128k vocabulary.\n\n**Costs:**\n\n- **Compilation.** Building the automaton and the token index for a new schema takes milliseconds to seconds. Cache compiled grammars by schema hash, or your first request per schema pays a visible TTFT penalty.\n- **Per-step masking.** With a precomputed index, small — a few hundred microseconds. Without one, it can dominate the decode step.\n- **Batching.** Each sequence may be in a different automaton state, so the mask is per-sequence. This is a scatter operation that does not batch as cleanly as the rest of the step.\n- **Quality.** Constraining can force the model down paths it assigns low probability, and there is evidence that aggressive constraints degrade reasoning quality. Prefer permissive schemas, and let the model emit a reasoning field before the constrained fields.\n\n**Jump-forward decoding** (XGrammar, SGLang) is the important optimisation: when the grammar admits only one continuation — the `":"` after a key name, the fixed field names of a schema — you can emit those tokens directly without a forward pass. On typical JSON this skips a large fraction of the steps and can make constrained generation *faster* than unconstrained.'
  );

  d('dr-057', 'debug', 'hard', '06 Decoding',
    'You enable speculative decoding and throughput drops 20% while TTFT is unchanged. What is happening?',
    'You are running at a batch size where speculation costs more than it saves, and you are measuring aggregate throughput rather than per-request latency.\n\nThe diagnosis path:\n\n1. **Check the acceptance rate.** Every engine exports it. Below ~0.5 the technique cannot pay for itself at any k. A common cause is a draft model from a different family or with a different tokenizer, or a draft that was not trained on your domain.\n\n2. **Check the running batch size.** If the mean batch is above roughly 32-64, the verification GEMM is on the compute-bound side and processing `B*k` positions genuinely costs `k` times more. Speculation converts spare compute into tokens; there is no spare compute.\n\n3. **Check k.** If k was set high (8, 16) on the assumption that more is better, and alpha is moderate, cost grows linearly while accepted tokens saturate. Sweep k and find the optimum.\n\n4. **Check the draft cost fraction.** A "small" draft that is 1/7 the size of the target is not 1/7 the cost at batch 1 — it has its own weight-streaming floor and its own kernel-launch overhead. Measure `c_draft` empirically rather than assuming it from parameter count.\n\n5. **Check whether the KV cache for the draft is competing** for memory with the target\u2019s, reducing the achievable batch size and therefore throughput independent of the speculation math.\n\nTTFT being unchanged is consistent with all of this: speculation touches only the decode loop.\n\n**Fix**: make speculation conditional on batch size, tune k against measured alpha, and if alpha is low, either fine-tune the draft on target outputs or switch to EAGLE-style feature-level drafting.'
  );

  d('dr-058', 'design', 'hard', '06 Decoding',
    'Design a decoding strategy for an agentic coding assistant: bursty single-user traffic, long shared prefixes, output is mostly code with heavy quotation from the context.',
    '**Workload reading**: batch is usually 1-4 (one developer, a few parallel tool calls), prefixes are heavily shared (same repo context, same system prompt across turns), and output copies substantially from the input. Every one of those is a lever.\n\n**Layer 1: prefix caching, aggressively.** The agent re-sends a large context each turn with a small delta. With a radix-tree cache the shared prefix is free after the first turn. This is the single largest win and it costs no accuracy. Size the cache generously and use an LRU that is aware of the session, so an active agent\u2019s prefix is not evicted by a different tenant.\n\n**Layer 2: prompt-lookup (n-gram) speculation, always on.** Code editing copies enormously — the model reproduces function signatures, variable names, and whole blocks from the context. Lookup speculation is free, has no memory cost, and on this workload plausibly delivers 2-3x. It should be the default.\n\n**Layer 3: EAGLE or a small same-family draft, for the non-copied portion.** Batch is small so speculation is in its ideal regime. Combine with layer 2 by trying lookup first and falling back to the model draft when no match is found. Set k from measured alpha, expect k in the 3-5 range.\n\n**Layer 4: structured decoding for tool calls**, with compiled grammars cached by schema hash and jump-forward enabled. Tool-call JSON is highly constrained, so jump-forward skips most of the tokens outright. Do *not* constrain the reasoning or the code body — let those be free, and constrain only the envelope.\n\n**What I would not do**: enable a large draft model and leave it on during batch bursts. Make speculation batch-conditional.\n\n**Measurement**: track acceptance rate split by source (lookup versus model draft), prefix cache hit tokens, and per-turn TTFT. The metric the user feels is time-to-first-token on turn N with a warm prefix, and end-to-end time for a full edit. Optimise those two directly.'
  );

  /* ---------------- Module 07: distributed ---------------- */
  d('dr-059', 'quant', 'hard', '07 Distributed',
    'Llama 3.1 70B with TP=8 on H100 NVLink (450 GB/s per-GPU bidirectional). Batch 32, hidden 8192, BF16. Compute the all-reduce cost per decode step and its share of the step.',
    'Two all-reduces per layer (one after attention output projection, one after the MLP down projection), 80 layers.\n\nRing all-reduce moves `2*(N-1)/N * S` bytes per GPU for a tensor of `S` bytes.\n\n```\nactivation per all-reduce = batch * hidden * 2 B = 32 * 8192 * 2 = 524,288 B\nbytes moved per GPU       = 2*(7/8) * 524,288 = 917,504 B\nall-reduces per step      = 80 * 2 = 160\ntotal per GPU per step    = 160 * 917,504 = 146.8 MB\ntime at 450 GB/s          = 146.8e6 / 450e9 = 0.33 ms\n```\n\nCompute side: 141 GB of weights spread over 8 GPUs = 17.6 GB each, at 3.35 TB/s = **5.3 ms**.\n\n```\nall-reduce share = 0.33 / (5.3 + 0.33) = 5.9%\n```\n\nAcceptable on NVLink. Now redo it on PCIe Gen5 at 64 GB/s effective:\n\n```\n146.8e6 / 64e9 = 2.29 ms  ->  30% of the step\n```\n\nAnd add latency: 160 all-reduces each with a fixed ~5-10 us launch and synchronisation cost is another 0.8-1.6 ms that the bandwidth model misses entirely. On PCIe, TP=8 is a bad idea. **This is why TP is confined to within a node and pipeline or expert parallelism is used across nodes.**\n\nAlso note the all-reduce cost is proportional to batch size while the weight-streaming cost is not — so the communication share *grows* with batch, and at batch 256 on NVLink it is closer to 30%.'
  );

  d('dr-060', 'concept', 'hard', '07 Distributed',
    'Contrast tensor, pipeline, and expert parallelism for inference specifically. Why is pipeline parallelism awkward for decode?',
    '**Tensor parallelism** splits each weight matrix across GPUs — column-wise for the first matrix of a pair, row-wise for the second, so exactly one all-reduce is needed per pair. Every GPU participates in every layer. It reduces both per-GPU memory *and* per-GPU latency, which is what you want for decode. Cost: an all-reduce per attention block and per MLP block, on the critical path, every step. Requires fast interconnect, so it is normally capped at the NVLink domain (8 GPUs).\n\n**Pipeline parallelism** splits layers across GPUs. Communication is tiny — one activation tensor between adjacent stages, point-to-point. Works fine over Ethernet. It reduces per-GPU memory but **not per-GPU latency**: a single token still traverses all stages sequentially, so single-stream latency is unchanged or worse.\n\nThe awkwardness for decode: in training you fill the pipeline with microbatches and the bubble amortises. In decode, the natural unit is one iteration producing one token per sequence, and there is nothing to fill the pipeline with unless you decompose the running batch into microbatches — which then interleave awkwardly with the iteration-level scheduler, and each stage\u2019s KV cache is for a different subset of layers, so a preemption or a new admission has to be coordinated across stages. Bubble fraction is `(P-1)/(M+P-1)` for P stages and M microbatches; with M small (which it is, because your batch is what it is) the bubble is large.\n\nSo: **PP is a memory-capacity tool for crossing node boundaries**, best used when the model genuinely does not fit and the interconnect cannot support TP. Combine as TP within node, PP across nodes.\n\n**Expert parallelism** applies to MoE: place different experts on different GPUs and route tokens to them. Communication is all-to-all, twice per MoE layer (dispatch and combine), with a volume proportional to `tokens * top_k * hidden`. It scales memory beautifully — 671B total parameters across many GPUs — but the all-to-all is latency-sensitive and load imbalance across experts directly becomes straggler time. DeepEP exists because the naive all-to-all is the bottleneck.'
  );

  d('dr-061', 'quant', 'medium', '07 Distributed',
    'DeepSeek-V3: 671B total parameters, 37B activated per token. Compute the memory needed and the decode step floor, and explain why the two numbers disagree so much.',
    '```\nweights (FP8)  : 671e9 * 1 B = 671 GB   -> needs >= 9 H100s just for weights\nweights (BF16) : 1342 GB                 -> ~17 H100s\n\nactivated per token: 37e9 params\nFLOPs per token    : 2 * 37e9 = 74 GFLOP\n```\n\nThe memory is set by *total* parameters because every expert must be resident somewhere; the compute is set by *activated* parameters because only `top_k` experts run per token.\n\nDecode step floor, batch 1, FP8 weights, 16 GPUs with expert parallelism:\n\n```\nbytes read per step (naive, all weights) = 671 GB / 16 = 42 GB per GPU -> 12.5 ms\nbytes actually read (only active experts) approx 37 GB total / 16 = 2.3 GB -> 0.7 ms\n```\n\nThe second number is the one that matters and is why MoE is attractive: you pay memory-capacity cost for 671B but bandwidth cost for 37B. The catch is that at batch 1 the routing is sparse and *different tokens hit different experts*, so with a large batch you end up touching most experts anyway, and the effective bytes read approach the dense number. MoE\u2019s bandwidth advantage is largest at small batch and erodes as batch grows — the opposite of most inference optimisations.\n\nAlso: DeepSeek-V3 uses MLA, so KV is `61 layers * 576 latent * 2 B = 68.6 KiB/token`, dramatically less than a dense model of comparable quality. That is a separate and independently large win.'
  );

  d('dr-062', 'concept', 'hard', '07 Distributed',
    'Explain prefill/decode disaggregation: what problem it solves, what it costs, and when it is not worth it.',
    '**The problem.** Prefill and decode have opposite resource profiles. Prefill is compute bound, benefits from large token batches, and wants high FLOPs. Decode is memory-bandwidth bound, benefits from many concurrent sequences, and wants high HBM bandwidth and capacity. Colocating them on the same replica means:\n\n1. Every prefill stalls the decodes sharing that GPU (the generation stall).\n2. You cannot tune the batch policy for both — the settings that maximise prefill throughput hurt decode latency.\n3. You cannot choose different hardware for the two phases.\n\n**The design.** Separate pools. Prefill workers run the prompt, produce the KV cache, transfer it to a decode worker, and the decode worker generates. TTFT and TPOT become independently tunable, and you can scale the pools independently to match your workload\u2019s prompt:completion ratio. DistServe reports large goodput gains under tight per-phase SLOs; Splitwise showed the hardware-heterogeneity angle (put prefill on newer compute-dense GPUs, decode on cheaper high-bandwidth ones); Mooncake built it around a KV store as the central abstraction.\n\n**The cost: the KV transfer.** For a 4000-token prompt on Llama 3.1 70B at 320 KiB/token, that is 1.28 GB per request. Over 400 Gb/s InfiniBand at 80% efficiency that is 32 ms added to TTFT. Over 100 Gb/s Ethernet it is 128 ms and the design is dead. **Disaggregation requires RDMA-class networking**, and the transfer must be layer-by-layer overlapped with prefill computation to hide it — which is what NIXL and LMCache exist to do.\n\n**When it is not worth it:**\n\n- Short prompts. If prompts are 200 tokens, prefill is 5 ms and there is nothing to disaggregate; you have added a network hop to save nothing.\n- Low traffic. Two under-utilised pools are worse than one well-utilised pool.\n- No RDMA fabric.\n- When chunked prefill already meets your SLO. Chunked prefill solves the stall problem with zero infrastructure; disaggregation solves it *and* gives independent scaling and heterogeneity, at considerable operational cost. Try the cheap fix first.'
  );

  d('dr-063', 'quant', 'medium', '07 Distributed',
    'You disaggregate prefill and decode. Prompts average 8000 tokens, outputs 500. Model is Llama 3.1 70B, TP=4 in each pool. How do you size the two pools?',
    'Work out the per-request cost in each phase.\n\n```\nPrefill: 2 * 70.6e9 * 8000 = 1.13 PFLOP\n  on 4x H100 at 989 TFLOP/s * 40% MFU = 1583 TFLOP/s effective\n  -> 1.13e15 / 1.583e15 = 0.71 s of prefill-pool time per request\n\nDecode: 500 tokens. Per step, weight bytes = 141 GB / 4 = 35.3 GB per GPU\n  at 3.35 TB/s * 0.8 = 13.2 ms per step (batch-independent floor)\n  With batch 64 the step is shared: 500 steps * 13.2 ms = 6.6 s of\n  decode-replica wall time, serving 64 sequences -> 0.103 s of\n  decode-pool time per request\n```\n\n```\nratio prefill:decode pool time = 0.71 : 0.103 = 6.9 : 1\n```\n\nSo you need roughly **7 prefill replicas per decode replica** at this prompt:completion ratio. That is a striking result and it is the point of the exercise: with 8000-token prompts and 500-token outputs, this workload is prefill-dominated, and a colocated deployment is spending most of its GPU time on prefill while its decode batching machinery sits underused.\n\nSanity checks to apply before believing it:\n\n- The decode number assumed batch 64 is achievable; verify against the KV budget.\n- Prefill MFU of 40% is optimistic for chunked execution; measure it.\n- Add the KV transfer: 8000 * 320 KiB = 2.5 GB per request, which at 400 Gb/s is 62 ms — 9% on top of prefill, and it consumes NIC bandwidth you must provision.\n- If the ratio flips (reasoning workloads with 200-token prompts and 8000-token outputs), the answer inverts to roughly 1:20 the other way. **Measure your own ratio; do not inherit someone else\u2019s.**'
  );

  d('dr-064', 'debug', 'hard', '07 Distributed',
    'An MoE deployment with expert parallelism shows 40% GPU idle time and high variance across ranks. Diagnose.',
    'Expert load imbalance, almost certainly.\n\nIn expert parallelism the all-to-all is a synchronisation point: every rank waits for the slowest. If the router sends 3x the average token count to the experts on rank 5, every other rank idles waiting for rank 5 to finish its expert FFNs. The symptom is exactly what you describe — high idle time, high variance across ranks, and throughput far below the sum of parts.\n\n**Confirm it**: export per-expert token counts per step. A healthy distribution is roughly uniform with modest variance; a pathological one has a few experts taking a large share. Routers are known to collapse toward a subset of experts, and the imbalance is often *input-dependent* — one language or one domain routes very differently from another.\n\n**Fixes, in increasing order of effort:**\n\n1. **Redundant experts / expert replication.** Replicate the hot experts on multiple ranks and split their tokens. DeepSeek\u2019s deployment does this and rebalances periodically based on observed load.\n2. **Better expert placement.** Rather than a fixed expert-to-rank map, place experts to equalise expected load using measured statistics. Needs periodic rebalancing as traffic shifts.\n3. **Capacity factor and token dropping.** Cap tokens per expert and drop or reroute the excess. Bounds the straggler at a quality cost — acceptable in training, questionable in inference.\n4. **Overlap the all-to-all with compute.** DeepEP and similar libraries pipeline the dispatch and combine against the expert computation, hiding some of the imbalance rather than eliminating it.\n\n**Also check the non-imbalance causes before committing:** an undersized all-to-all message that is latency- rather than bandwidth-bound; a NIC mapped to the wrong NUMA node; and whether the "idle" is actually the shared-expert path serialising against the routed path.'
  );

  d('dr-065', 'concept', 'medium', '07 Distributed',
    'Why does tensor parallelism give sub-linear latency scaling, and what is the practical limit?',
    'TP=N divides the weight bytes per GPU by N, so the compute portion of the step scales as `1/N`. But it *adds* an all-reduce whose cost is roughly constant in N (ring all-reduce moves `2(N-1)/N * S` bytes per GPU, which approaches `2S` and stops shrinking) and whose *latency* component grows with N because a ring has N-1 steps.\n\n```\nstep(N) = compute/N + comm_bw + comm_latency*(N-1)\n```\n\nThe first term shrinks, the second is flat, the third grows. There is therefore an N beyond which latency gets worse, and it is closer than people expect for small models.\n\nConcretely, Llama 3.1 8B on H100: at TP=1 the step floor is 4.8 ms; at TP=8 the compute term is 0.6 ms while 160 all-reduces at ~8 us of fixed latency each contribute 1.3 ms. You get maybe 3x from an 8x resource increase, and per-GPU efficiency has fallen to 37%.\n\n**Practical limits:**\n\n- TP must stay within the fast-interconnect domain. On an 8-GPU NVLink node, TP<=8. Crossing to another node over Ethernet is catastrophic.\n- TP degree must divide the number of attention heads (and for GQA, ideally the number of KV heads — otherwise KV heads are replicated, which wastes memory).\n- Small models on large TP waste GPUs. Prefer replicating the model N times over TP=N if it fits, since N independent replicas give N times the throughput while TP=N gives maybe 3x.\n\n**The decision rule**: use the smallest TP that makes the model fit with an adequate KV budget, then replicate. Increase TP beyond that only when single-stream latency is the binding SLO and you are willing to pay for it.'
  );

  /* ---------------- Module 08: production ---------------- */
  d('dr-066', 'design', 'hard', '08 Production',
    'Design a cache-aware router for a fleet of 16 replicas serving a chat product with long shared system prompts.',
    '**Objective**: maximise prefix-cache hit rate without creating hotspots, and degrade gracefully when a replica is overloaded.\n\n**Core mechanism: consistent hashing on the prompt prefix.** Hash the first K tokens (or the session ID, if the product has one) to select a replica, so the same conversation and the same system prompt land on the same replica whose radix tree already holds them. This turns a fleet-wide 1/16 hit rate into something near the single-replica hit rate.\n\n**Load awareness is mandatory**, because pure hashing creates hotspots the moment one system prompt is more popular than the others. Use "power of two choices with cache affinity": compute the two best replicas by cache affinity, pick the less loaded of the two. Load here should be *queue depth and KV utilisation*, not GPU utilisation.\n\n**Overflow policy.** If the affinity replica exceeds a load threshold, fall back to the least-loaded replica and accept the cache miss. Recomputing a prefill is expensive but bounded; queueing behind an overloaded replica is not.\n\n**Signals the router needs from each replica**, scraped every 1-2 seconds or pushed: number of waiting requests, number of running requests, KV cache utilisation, and ideally a bloom filter or sketch of cached prefix hashes. vLLM\u2019s production stack and SGLang\u2019s router both do versions of this.\n\n**Session stickiness** with an explicit TTL, so a multi-turn conversation stays put for its lifetime and then releases.\n\n**What to avoid**: round-robin, which destroys cache locality; least-connections without cache awareness, which does the same; and sticky routing without an overflow valve, which converts a hot tenant into an outage.\n\n**Measure**: fleet-wide prefix cache hit *token* rate (not request rate), per-replica load skew, and the fraction of requests that took the overflow path. If overflow is common, you need more replicas or a smaller hash granularity.'
  );

  d('dr-067', 'quant', 'hard', '08 Production',
    'Cold start for a 70B model is 6 minutes. Traffic doubles over 3 minutes. Show why reactive autoscaling fails and quantify what you need instead.',
    '```\nt=0    : traffic starts rising, 10 replicas at 70% utilisation\nt=90s  : threshold crossed, scale-up triggered\nt=90s  : new pods scheduled, image pull + weight load begins\nt=450s : new replicas ready\n```\n\nBetween t=90s and t=450s — **six minutes** — you serve doubled traffic on the original capacity. Utilisation goes to 140% of capacity, the queue grows without bound, and by the time the new replicas arrive you have a backlog that takes further minutes to drain. Every request in that window misses its SLO. Reactive autoscaling with a 6-minute cold start is not autoscaling, it is a post-mortem generator.\n\n**What actually works, in order of impact:**\n\n1. **Attack the 6 minutes.** Weight loading dominates: 141 GB from object storage at 1 GB/s is 141 s, and container image pull for a multi-GB CUDA image is often worse. Fixes: cache images on the node, stream weights from local NVMe or a node-local cache (run:ai model-streamer, or a DaemonSet that pre-pulls), use safetensors with memory-mapped loading, and pre-warm CUDA graph capture. Getting to 60-90 s is achievable and changes the whole calculus.\n\n2. **Provision for the peak, not the mean.** With a slow scale-up, headroom is the only real defence. Size for p99 traffic plus a margin, and accept the idle cost as the price of the SLO.\n\n3. **Predictive scaling.** Traffic is usually diurnal and highly predictable. Scale on a forecast, an hour ahead, not on a threshold.\n\n4. **Warm pool.** Keep N replicas loaded and idle (or running low-priority batch work that can be preempted instantly). Converts a 6-minute scale-up into a seconds-long one at the cost of the warm capacity.\n\n5. **Load shedding as the backstop.** When the queue exceeds what current capacity can drain within SLO, return 429 immediately. Shedding 10% of traffic cleanly is strictly better than missing SLO on 100%.\n\n**Scale on the right signal**: queue depth and SLO attainment, with `scale_up_stabilization` short and `scale_down_stabilization` long (10+ minutes), because flapping a 6-minute-cold-start service is ruinous.'
  );

  d('dr-068', 'concept', 'hard', '08 Production',
    'Why is GPU utilisation a misleading autoscaling signal for LLM serving, and what should you use?',
    '`nvidia-smi` utilisation reports the fraction of time *at least one kernel was resident*. During decode, kernels are resident essentially all the time — the GPU is streaming weights continuously — so utilisation pins near 100% whether you are serving 1 sequence or 200. It measures occupancy of the timeline, not consumption of any resource that is actually scarce.\n\nConcretely, a replica at batch 1 and a replica at batch 128 both report ~95-100% utilisation. Their throughput differs by more than 50x. Scaling on this signal means scaling on noise.\n\n**What to scale on:**\n\n1. **Queue depth / number of waiting requests.** The most direct leading indicator that demand exceeds capacity. It rises *before* latency does.\n2. **SLO attainment** — the fraction of requests meeting the TTFT and TPOT targets over a rolling window. This is what you actually care about; scale to keep it above target.\n3. **KV cache utilisation.** Above ~85% you are near preemption, which is the point where latency degrades non-linearly. An excellent scale-up trigger.\n4. **Time-to-first-token p95** specifically, since it captures queueing directly.\n\n**Secondary signals worth exporting**: running batch size (tells you whether you are efficiency-limited), preemption rate (tells you you are already over), and arrival rate (for predictive scaling).\n\nIf you must use a hardware counter, **achieved memory bandwidth** or SM occupancy from DCGM is far more informative than `utilization.gpu`, because it does distinguish batch 1 from batch 128. But queue depth is simpler and closer to the SLO.'
  );

  d('dr-069', 'debug', 'hard', '08 Production',
    'p99 latency degrades every day at the same time, but traffic is flat. Investigate.',
    'Flat traffic with a time-correlated regression points away from load and toward something else that is on a schedule.\n\nCandidates, roughly in order of likelihood:\n\n1. **Workload mix shift, not volume.** Total requests flat, but a batch job with 32k-token prompts starts at that hour. Prompt length distribution changes, KV pressure rises, preemption starts. Check p95 prompt length and generated length over time, not just request count — this is by far the most common answer and it is invisible if you only chart RPS.\n\n2. **Cache eviction from a different tenant.** A daily job with a different system prompt floods the radix cache, evicting the interactive tenant\u2019s prefixes. Prefix cache hit rate will show the dip exactly.\n\n3. **Co-tenancy on the node.** A logging agent, a backup, a security scan, or a Kubernetes cron job consuming CPU or PCIe bandwidth. Check host CPU steal, PCIe counters, and node-level cron.\n\n4. **Thermal or power.** A datacentre cooling cycle or a power cap. DCGM exports SM clock and power; a clock drop at the same time every day is conclusive.\n\n5. **Autoscaler scale-down.** If the scale-down stabilisation window is short, the fleet shrinks during a brief lull and then runs hot. Correlate replica count with the regression.\n\n6. **Log rotation, metric scrape amplification, or a checkpoint sync** doing large I/O on the same host.\n\n**Method**: overlay the p99 series with prompt-length percentiles, prefix cache hit rate, replica count, SM clock, and KV utilisation on one dashboard for a 48-hour window. The one that moves in lockstep is the cause. If none does, capture an Nsight or per-iteration scheduler trace during the window and compare against a healthy window.'
  );

  d('dr-070', 'design', 'hard', '08 Production',
    'Design multi-tenancy for 200 customers on a shared LLM fleet, with per-customer fairness and no noisy-neighbour failures.',
    '**The unit of fairness is tokens, not requests.** A request can be 100 tokens or 100,000. Any scheme that counts requests will be gamed within a day. Virtual Token Counter (VTC) formalises this: track cumulative service in tokens per tenant and schedule the tenant with the least service, weighted by entitlement.\n\n**Layers:**\n\n1. **Quotas at the edge.** Per-tenant tokens-per-minute and concurrent-request limits, enforced with a token bucket before anything touches a GPU. Return 429 with `Retry-After`. This is the only layer that reliably contains abuse.\n\n2. **Fair scheduling inside the engine.** Weighted fair queueing over tenants on token service. Guarantees that a tenant sending 10x the traffic gets its share, not 10x the share.\n\n3. **KV budget reservation per tier.** Without this, one tenant\u2019s long contexts fill the cache and everyone else gets preempted. Reserve a floor per tier and let the remainder be contended.\n\n4. **Isolation by pool for the tail.** Do not try to make one pool serve a 1M-token-per-minute customer and 199 small ones. Give the largest customers dedicated replicas — it is simpler and it removes the hardest fairness cases. Shared pools for the long tail.\n\n5. **Per-tenant limits on the dangerous parameters**: `max_tokens`, `max_model_len`, and whether guided decoding with arbitrary grammars is allowed (a pathological grammar is a denial-of-service vector).\n\n6. **Multi-LoRA for per-tenant customisation** rather than per-tenant replicas — S-LoRA and Punica show thousands of adapters on one base model, with adapter weights paged in and batched heterogeneously. This is what makes 200 tenants economically viable at all.\n\n**Observability**: per-tenant TTFT, TPOT, token throughput, rejection rate and share of service. Alert when any tenant\u2019s share of service exceeds its entitlement for a sustained window — that is your noisy-neighbour detector.\n\n**Failure containment**: a per-tenant circuit breaker, and the ability to drain one tenant to a quarantine pool without redeploying.'
  );

  d('dr-071', 'concept', 'medium', '08 Production',
    'What breaks first when you extend a deployment from 8k to 128k context, and how do you handle each?',
    '**1. KV memory, immediately.** At 320 KiB/token (70B), 128k tokens is 41 GB *for one sequence*. Your batch size collapses from 64 to 1. Handle with: FP8 KV (2x), GQA/MLA models chosen for this purpose, KV offload to host or NVMe for inactive sequences, and eviction/compression schemes (H2O, SnapKV, StreamingLLM) if quality permits.\n\n**2. Prefill time and TTFT.** 128k prefill on 70B: `2 * 70.6e9 * 128,000 = 18.1 PFLOP`, plus attention which is now genuinely quadratic and no longer negligible — at 128k the attention term is `4 * 80 * 128,000^2 * 8192` which exceeds the linear term. Expect tens of seconds. Handle with: chunked prefill (mandatory, or every other request stalls), prefix caching if there is reuse, and parallel prefill across GPUs (sequence/context parallelism) if TTFT must be bounded.\n\n**3. Attention kernel behaviour.** Decode attention now reads 41 GB of KV per step for that one sequence, so the sequence is entirely KV bound and the weights are irrelevant. FlashDecoding-style split-K along the sequence dimension is required to get parallelism; without it a single long sequence cannot fill the GPU.\n\n**4. Scheduling fairness.** One 128k request can monopolise the cache. Needs per-request KV caps and admission control that accounts for projected KV, not current KV.\n\n**5. Quality.** Effective context is often far below advertised context. Validate with RULER at your target length before promising it. Position-extrapolation methods (YaRN, rope scaling) have real quality costs that needle-in-a-haystack will not reveal.\n\n**6. Cost.** A 128k prefill is roughly 16x a 8k prefill in FLOPs plus a superlinear attention term. Price it explicitly, cache aggressively, and consider whether retrieval would serve the use case at 1% of the cost.'
  );

  d('dr-072', 'design', 'medium', '08 Production',
    'Write the rollout plan for upgrading the serving engine version on a fleet serving production traffic.',
    '**Pre-flight.**\n\n- Pin the exact image digest, not a tag. Record engine version, model revision, and the full serving config in one artefact.\n- Diff the default configuration between versions. Engine defaults change between minor releases (`max_num_seqs`, attention backend, chunked prefill enablement, KV dtype) and a silent default change is the most common cause of an "unexplained" regression.\n- Run the offline benchmark suite on the new version at the exact production config, and the quality eval suite. Compare against the recorded baseline with confidence intervals.\n\n**Shadow.**\n\n- Mirror a sample of production traffic to a canary replica on the new version, discarding responses. Compare TTFT, TPOT, throughput and error rate. This catches performance regressions with zero user risk.\n- For output-quality risk, log paired outputs from old and new on the same prompts and diff them offline. Numerical changes (a new attention backend, a different kernel) will produce different tokens; you need to decide in advance whether that is acceptable.\n\n**Canary.**\n\n- 1% of live traffic, one hour, with automatic rollback on SLO attainment, error rate, or preemption rate breaching thresholds.\n- Then 5%, 25%, 50%, 100% with a soak at each step long enough to cover a full traffic cycle if possible.\n\n**Guardrails.**\n\n- Keep the previous version deployable in one command; never delete the old image until a full week has passed.\n- Freeze other changes during the rollout so attribution is unambiguous.\n- Do not roll out during peak, and do not roll out on a Friday.\n\n**Post.**\n\n- Re-record the performance baseline at the new version. A baseline you do not update becomes a lie within two releases.'
  );

  /* ---------------- Module 09: economics and frontier ---------------- */
  d('dr-073', 'quant', 'hard', '09 Economics',
    'H100 at $2.50/hour. Llama 3.1 8B, batch 64, measured 2100 output tokens/second per GPU. Compute cost per million output tokens and compare with API pricing.',
    '```\ntokens per hour = 2100 * 3600 = 7.56e6\ncost per Mtok   = 2.50 / 7.56 = $0.331\n```\n\nThat is the raw compute cost at 100% utilisation. Now apply reality:\n\n```\nutilisation 45% (diurnal traffic, headroom for SLO)\n  -> $0.331 / 0.45 = $0.735 per Mtok\n\n+ replication for HA (1.3x)          -> $0.956\n+ ingress/egress, storage, control   -> ~ +10% -> $1.05\n+ engineering amortisation           -> workload-dependent\n```\n\nAgainst public API pricing for an 8B-class model of roughly $0.05-$0.20 per million output tokens, self-hosting this model is **5-20x more expensive**.\n\nThe conclusions worth drawing:\n\n1. **Utilisation dominates.** The gap between $0.33 and $0.74 is entirely utilisation. Any self-hosting business case lives or dies on keeping the GPUs busy — which means batch workloads to fill the troughs, or multi-tenancy, or reserved capacity sized to the floor with burst to an API.\n2. **Providers win on scale**, aggregating many customers into high utilisation, and on hardware cost far below $2.50/hour.\n3. **Self-hosting is justified by non-cost reasons**: data residency, latency to your own infrastructure, custom models or LoRAs, guaranteed capacity, and predictable pricing. Justify it on those, and treat cost parity as a bonus.\n4. **Always compute the input-token cost separately.** Prefill and decode have different cost structures and a 40:1 prompt:completion workload has a completely different economics than a 1:10 one.'
  );

  d('dr-074', 'quant', 'medium', '09 Economics',
    'A reasoning model generates 8000 thinking tokens plus a 200-token answer. Compare the cost against a non-reasoning model that answers in 200 tokens, and say when it is worth it.',
    '```\nreasoning    : 8200 output tokens\nnon-reasoning:  200 output tokens\nratio        : 41x more output tokens\n```\n\nAnd output tokens are the expensive ones — they are generated one at a time in the memory-bound regime, while input tokens are processed in a compute-efficient prefill.\n\n```\nat $2 per Mtok output:\n  reasoning     : 8200 * 2e-6  = $0.0164 per request\n  non-reasoning :  200 * 2e-6  = $0.0004 per request\n```\n\nAlso the latency: at 50 tok/s, 8200 tokens is **164 seconds**. That is an entirely different product experience.\n\n**When it is worth it**: when the task has a verifiable correct answer that the non-reasoning model gets wrong. If reasoning takes accuracy from 60% to 90% on a task where a wrong answer costs more than $0.016 of downstream human correction, it is trivially worth it. Mathematics, code that must compile, multi-step data extraction, planning.\n\n**When it is not**: chat, summarisation, classification, retrieval-grounded question answering — anywhere the non-reasoning model is already near ceiling. Paying 41x for a 1-point gain is indefensible.\n\n**The systems consequence, which is the interesting part**: test-time scaling shifts the entire workload from prefill-heavy to decode-heavy. A serving stack tuned for 40:1 summarisation is wrong for 1:40 reasoning. Decode throughput, KV capacity for long generations, and speculative decoding all become far more valuable; prefill FLOPs become less so. This is why the hardware conversation has moved toward memory bandwidth and capacity.\n\n**The lever nobody uses enough**: cap the thinking budget. Most reasoning gains are captured in the first fraction of the thinking tokens, and a budget-forcing stop is a direct cost control.'
  );

  d('dr-075', 'concept', 'hard', '09 Economics',
    'Build the full unit-economics model for a serving deployment. What must be in it, and what do people leave out?',
    '**Revenue side**: price per million input tokens and per million output tokens, and the actual observed mix. Do not model a single blended token price; the prompt:completion ratio varies enormously by customer and it is the main driver of margin dispersion.\n\n**Cost side, per replica-hour:**\n\n- GPU instance cost (on-demand, reserved, or spot with an interruption model).\n- The other GPUs in the replica — a TP=8 replica costs 8 GPUs even if only one is on your dashboard.\n- Storage for weights and the egress to load them on every scale-up.\n- Networking, especially if disaggregated (RDMA fabric is not free).\n- Control plane, monitoring, logging (token-level logging at scale is a real line item).\n\n**Throughput side, which is where the modelling actually happens:**\n\n- Output tokens/second/replica **at your SLO**, not at maximum batch. This is the number people substitute wrongly: max-throughput benchmarks are typically 2-4x the SLO-constrained figure.\n- Prefill tokens/second/replica, costed separately.\n- Effective utilisation over the day, including the diurnal trough.\n\n**Commonly omitted, in the order they cause surprises:**\n\n1. **Utilisation.** Modelling at 100% understates cost by 2-3x.\n2. **Headroom for SLO.** You cannot run at 95% utilisation and meet a p95 latency target; the headroom is a cost.\n3. **Replication for availability.** N+1 or N+2 at minimum, plus multi-region if you promise it.\n4. **Cold-start capacity** held warm.\n5. **Failed and cancelled requests**, which consume GPU time and produce no revenue. Client-cancelled streams in chat products are a surprisingly large fraction.\n6. **Retries** on transient errors.\n7. **The evaluation and canary fleet.**\n8. **Engineering cost.** A team keeping this running is often larger than the hardware bill for small deployments.\n\n**The output should be a sensitivity table**, not a point estimate: cost per Mtok as a function of utilisation, batch size at SLO, and prompt:completion ratio. Those three explain almost all the variance.'
  );

  d('dr-076', 'concept', 'medium', '09 Economics',
    'Batch/offline inference can be 50% cheaper than real-time. Why, mechanically?',
    'Because every constraint that costs you money in real-time serving is a latency constraint, and batch has none.\n\n1. **Batch size.** Real-time caps `max_num_seqs` to keep TPOT within SLO. Offline can run at the maximum the KV cache supports — often 4-8x larger — and in the memory-bound regime throughput scales nearly linearly with batch until you hit the ridge point. This alone is most of the difference.\n\n2. **Utilisation.** Real-time must be provisioned for peak with headroom, so mean utilisation is 40-60%. Batch runs at 100% by construction: the queue is always full.\n\n3. **Scheduling freedom.** Offline can sort requests by length to minimise padding and maximise batch homogeneity, group by shared prefix to maximise cache hits, and run prefill and decode in whatever mix is most efficient. Real-time takes what arrives.\n\n4. **Spot/preemptible instances.** A batch job can checkpoint and resume, so it can run on interruptible capacity at 30-70% discount. A latency-SLO service cannot.\n\n5. **Trough filling.** Batch work can be scheduled into the diurnal gaps of the real-time fleet, making its marginal hardware cost close to zero.\n\n6. **No headroom, no warm pool, no cold-start reserve.**\n\nThis is exactly why the major API providers offer a batch tier at ~50% of the synchronous price with a 24-hour turnaround — the discount is real cost, not a marketing subsidy.\n\n**The operational lesson**: separate your workloads. Anything that does not need a response within seconds — evaluation, enrichment, embedding generation, offline classification, synthetic data — should go through a batch path. Mixing it into the real-time fleet imports its latency requirements onto work that does not need them.'
  );

  d('dr-077', 'concept', 'hard', '09 Economics',
    'The hardware trend is FLOPs growing faster than memory bandwidth. What does that imply for inference system design over the next few years?',
    'The ridge point `B_crit = FLOPs/bandwidth` has risen steadily: roughly 150 on A100 BF16, 295 on H100 BF16, 590 on H100 FP8, and higher still on Blackwell with FP4. Decode at a given batch size therefore moves *further* into the memory-bound region with each generation — you get less of the advertised FLOPs improvement than the spec sheet suggests.\n\nImplications, in order of importance:\n\n1. **Anything that reduces bytes moved per token becomes more valuable over time**, and anything that only reduces FLOPs becomes less valuable. That is the strategic case for KV compression (MLA, GQA, quantised KV, eviction), for weight quantisation, and for architectural changes that shrink the KV cache.\n\n2. **Batch size must grow to reach the same efficiency**, which requires more KV capacity, which is itself bandwidth- and capacity-constrained. The two pressures compound. Expect KV cache management to remain the central systems problem.\n\n3. **Speculative decoding gets structurally more attractive**, because it converts idle FLOPs into tokens, and the idle FLOPs fraction is growing. Expect the acceptance-rate literature (EAGLE and successors) to keep mattering.\n\n4. **MoE gets more attractive**: it decouples parameter count (memory capacity) from activated parameters (bandwidth per token). Given HBM capacity growth (80 -> 141 -> 192 -> 288 GB) outpacing bandwidth growth in relative terms, storing more and reading less is the right trade.\n\n5. **Disaggregation and heterogeneity**: prefill wants FLOPs, decode wants bandwidth, and the two are diverging. Running them on different hardware becomes more rational each generation, which is the Splitwise thesis.\n\n6. **Interconnect becomes the scaling axis.** NVLink domains growing to 72 GPUs (GB200 NVL72) is a response to exactly this: if you cannot feed a GPU from its own HBM, feed it from the domain.\n\n**The counter-trend to watch**: HBM4 and larger stacks, and the possibility that memory-centric architectures (or wafer-scale, or large SRAM designs like Groq/Cerebras) change the ratio in the other direction for inference specifically. The ratio is the number to track, not either term alone.'
  );

  d('dr-078', 'design', 'hard', '09 Economics',
    'Capstone: you are handed a serving deployment costing $180k/month at p95 TTFT 1.2s (SLO 500ms) and p95 TPOT 45ms (SLO 25ms). Give your first two weeks.',
    '**Days 1-3: measure, change nothing.**\n\n- Establish the workload distribution: prompt length percentiles, output length percentiles, arrival pattern over a week, prompt:completion ratio, prefix-sharing structure.\n- Record the full serving configuration, engine version, and hardware.\n- Decompose TTFT into queue time and prefill time. This single split usually determines the whole plan: queue-dominated means a capacity or scheduling problem, prefill-dominated means a compute or caching problem.\n- Instrument per-iteration scheduler timings and check preemption rate and KV utilisation.\n- Compute the theoretical floor for decode step time and prefill time from first principles, and compare to measured. The ratio tells you how much is available from tuning versus how much needs hardware or model changes.\n\n**Days 4-7: the free wins, in order.**\n\n1. If TPOT p95 is 45ms against a p50 that is much lower, it is almost certainly unchunked prefill. **Enable chunked prefill** and tune `max_num_batched_tokens`. This is typically the largest single improvement and costs nothing.\n2. **Enable prefix caching** if there is any shared prefix. Chat with a system prompt: expect a large prefill reduction.\n3. **Check `enforce_eager`** and CUDA graph capture. 30-50% is routinely left on the floor here.\n4. **Fix `max_num_seqs` and `gpu_memory_utilization`.** Defaults are rarely right. If preemption rate is nonzero, `max_num_seqs` is too high for the KV budget.\n5. Re-measure after each change independently. Batched changes cannot be attributed.\n\n**Days 8-11: the cheap wins.**\n\n- **FP8 KV cache** if on Hopper: 2x KV capacity, larger batch, faster step. Validate on RULER and the eval suite.\n- **FP8 weights and activations** if the accuracy budget allows: 2x on the GEMMs and half the weight bytes.\n- **Cache-aware routing** across replicas if the fleet is round-robin today.\n- **Speculative decoding** if the batch is small; prompt-lookup first since it is free.\n\n**Days 12-14: the structural decisions, now informed.**\n\n- Right-size TP. If the model fits at TP=2, running TP=8 is burning 4x the hardware for maybe 2x the latency.\n- Decide on disaggregation only if prompts are long, traffic is high, and RDMA exists — and only after chunked prefill has failed to meet SLO.\n- Autoscaling on queue depth and SLO attainment rather than GPU utilisation; fix cold start if it is over two minutes.\n- Move any latency-insensitive traffic to a batch path.\n\n**Deliverable**: a before/after table with cost per million output tokens, p95 TTFT and TPOT, and utilisation, plus the attribution of each change. My expectation from experience is that configuration and caching alone close most of the SLO gap and 30-50% of the cost, before any quantisation or architectural change is considered.\n\n**What I would refuse to do on day 1**: buy more GPUs. A deployment missing SLO by 2.4x on TTFT with defaults unexamined is a tuning problem until proven otherwise.'
  );

  /* ==== DRILLS ==== */

  window.DRILLS = D;
})();
